-- Flashcard top-up bundle: 1-sciences (17 decks)
BEGIN;

-- Flashcard top-up: Biology|AQA|alevel — brings each topic up to 30 cards.
-- Generated by scripts/build-flashcard-topup.js from flashcards/topup/src/biology-aqa-alevel.txt
-- Safe to re-run: skips fronts already present and never takes a topic past 30.
WITH v(subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, ord) AS (VALUES
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'What is a monomer and a polymer?', 'A monomer is a small unit that can join with others; a polymer is a large molecule made of many monomers joined together.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'What is a glycosidic bond and how is it formed?', 'A covalent bond between two monosaccharides, formed by a condensation reaction that releases a molecule of water.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'Which monosaccharides form maltose, sucrose and lactose?', 'Maltose: glucose + glucose. Sucrose: glucose + fructose. Lactose: glucose + galactose.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'How does glycogen''s structure suit its function?', 'It is highly branched, so it can be hydrolysed rapidly at many ends to release glucose. It is insoluble and compact, so it is a good store in animals.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'How does cellulose''s structure give plant cell walls strength?', 'β-glucose chains are straight and linked by many hydrogen bonds into microfibrils, which provide high tensile strength.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'How is a test for non-reducing sugars carried out?', 'After a negative Benedict''s test, boil with dilute HCl, neutralise with sodium hydrogencarbonate, then repeat Benedict''s. A brick-red precipitate shows a non-reducing sugar.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'What is a peptide bond and how is it formed?', 'A bond between the amine group of one amino acid and the carboxyl group of another, formed by condensation.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'Give the general structure of an amino acid.', 'A central carbon bonded to an amine group (–NH2), a carboxyl group (–COOH), a hydrogen atom and a variable R group.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'Why does temperature above the optimum reduce enzyme activity?', 'Hydrogen and ionic bonds in the tertiary structure break, changing the active site shape so substrates no longer fit (denaturation).', 9),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'How does pH affect enzyme activity?', 'Changes in H+ concentration alter the charges on amino acids in the active site and break ionic and hydrogen bonds, changing its shape.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'How does substrate concentration affect the rate of an enzyme-controlled reaction?', 'Rate increases as more enzyme–substrate complexes form, until all active sites are occupied; then enzyme concentration becomes limiting.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'What is the role of DNA helicase and DNA polymerase in replication?', 'Helicase breaks hydrogen bonds between bases to unwind the strands. DNA polymerase joins free nucleotides by condensation, forming phosphodiester bonds.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'How did Meselson and Stahl show DNA replication is semi-conservative?', 'Bacteria grown in 15N were transferred to 14N. After one generation, DNA was all intermediate density; after two, half intermediate and half light.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'Why is ATP a suitable immediate energy source?', 'It releases energy in small, manageable amounts; hydrolysis is a single step; it is easily resynthesised; and it can phosphorylate other molecules.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Biological Molecules', 'What are the properties of water that matter to living organisms?', 'High specific heat capacity (buffers temperature), high latent heat of vaporisation (cooling), a good solvent, cohesion (water columns in xylem) and a metabolite.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Cells', 'What is the function of the mitochondria?', 'The site of aerobic respiration (link reaction, Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation), producing ATP.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Cells', 'What is the function of the Golgi apparatus?', 'It modifies proteins (e.g. adding carbohydrates), packages them into vesicles, and forms lysosomes.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Cells', 'What are plasmids and why are they important in bacteria?', 'Small circular DNA loops separate from the main chromosome. They often carry genes such as antibiotic resistance and can be transferred between bacteria.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Cells', 'How do viruses differ from living cells?', 'They are acellular and non-living: genetic material (DNA or RNA) in a capsid, sometimes with an envelope and attachment proteins. They can only replicate inside host cells.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Cells', 'How do prokaryotic cells divide?', 'By binary fission: the circular DNA and plasmids replicate, the cell grows, and the cytoplasm divides into two daughter cells.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Cells', 'What is the role of cholesterol in cell membranes?', 'It binds to phospholipid tails, reducing fluidity and making the membrane more stable.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Cells', 'What is the role of glycoproteins and glycolipids in membranes?', 'Cell recognition and signalling (as receptors or antigens), and helping cells attach to each other.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Cells', 'How does temperature affect membrane permeability?', 'Above about 40 °C, phospholipids gain kinetic energy and proteins denature, making the membrane more permeable.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Cells', 'What is osmosis?', 'The net movement of water from a region of higher water potential to a region of lower water potential across a partially permeable membrane.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Cells', 'What are the factors affecting the rate of diffusion?', 'Surface area, concentration gradient, thickness of the exchange surface, and temperature.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Cells', 'What is an antigen?', 'A molecule (usually a protein) on the surface of a cell or virus that triggers an immune response.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Cells', 'What is a monoclonal antibody and give one use?', 'Identical antibodies from a single clone of B cells, binding to one specific antigen. Used in pregnancy tests, targeting cancer drugs, and ELISA.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Cells', 'What happens in phagocytosis?', 'A phagocyte engulfs a pathogen into a phagosome; a lysosome fuses with it and lysozymes hydrolyse the pathogen; antigens may be presented on the phagocyte''s surface.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'How is surface area to volume ratio calculated and why does it matter?', 'Surface area ÷ volume. As organisms get larger the ratio decreases, so diffusion across the body surface is too slow to meet their needs.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'Describe the pathway of air into the lungs.', 'Trachea → bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'Describe the mechanism of inspiration.', 'External intercostal muscles contract and the diaphragm contracts and flattens. Thorax volume increases, pressure falls below atmospheric, and air moves in.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'Define tidal volume and ventilation rate.', 'Tidal volume: the volume of air breathed in or out in one normal breath. Ventilation rate: breaths per minute. Pulmonary ventilation = tidal volume × ventilation rate.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'How does emphysema affect gas exchange?', 'Alveolar walls break down, reducing surface area, and elastin is lost, reducing recoil. Less oxygen diffuses into the blood.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'What is the role of bile in lipid digestion?', 'Bile salts emulsify lipids into small droplets, increasing surface area for lipase.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'How are monoglycerides and fatty acids absorbed?', 'Micelles carry them to the epithelial cells, where they diffuse in and are reformed into triglycerides, packaged into chylomicrons and enter the lacteals.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'What is the Bohr effect?', 'Higher CO2 concentration lowers blood pH, reducing haemoglobin''s affinity for oxygen. The dissociation curve shifts right, so more oxygen is unloaded to respiring tissues.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'Why do small mammals have haemoglobin with a lower affinity for oxygen?', 'They have a high metabolic rate, so their haemoglobin needs to unload oxygen readily to respiring tissues.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'How is cardiac output calculated?', 'Cardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'What is the function of the coronary arteries?', 'To supply the heart muscle with oxygenated blood.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'Why do veins have valves?', 'Blood is at low pressure, so valves prevent backflow and keep blood returning to the heart.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'What are the roles of the atrioventricular and semilunar valves?', 'AV valves prevent backflow from ventricles to atria. Semilunar valves prevent backflow from the arteries into the ventricles.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'What is root pressure?', 'Active transport of mineral ions into the xylem lowers the water potential, so water enters by osmosis and pushes water up the xylem.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Organisms Exchange Substances', 'What evidence supports the mass-flow hypothesis?', 'Sap is released when phloem is cut; the concentration of sucrose is higher in leaves than roots; ringing a stem causes swelling above the ring; aphid stylets show pressure.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'What is a codon and an anticodon?', 'Codon: a sequence of three mRNA bases coding for an amino acid. Anticodon: the complementary three bases on a tRNA molecule.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'What does it mean that the genetic code is degenerate?', 'More than one codon codes for the same amino acid.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'What are introns and exons?', 'Exons are the coding sequences of a gene; introns are non-coding sequences that are removed from pre-mRNA by splicing.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'Describe the structure of tRNA.', 'A single-stranded, cloverleaf-shaped RNA with an anticodon at one end and an amino acid binding site at the other, held by hydrogen bonds.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'What is the role of RNA polymerase in transcription?', 'It joins free RNA nucleotides complementary to the template DNA strand, forming phosphodiester bonds to make pre-mRNA.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'What is a mutagenic agent? Give two examples.', 'A factor that increases the rate of gene mutation, e.g. ionising radiation (X-rays, UV) and chemicals such as benzene.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'What is a frameshift mutation?', 'An addition or deletion of a base (not a multiple of three) that shifts the reading frame, changing every codon after it.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'What is non-disjunction?', 'The failure of chromosomes or chromatids to separate in meiosis, giving gametes with an extra or missing chromosome (e.g. Down''s syndrome).', 8),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'How does crossing over produce variation?', 'Homologous chromosomes pair up and exchange sections of chromatids at chiasmata, producing new combinations of alleles.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'How does independent segregation produce variation?', 'Homologous pairs line up randomly at the equator in meiosis I, so different combinations of maternal and paternal chromosomes go into each gamete.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'How many different gamete combinations can independent segregation produce?', '2^n, where n is the number of homologous pairs (2^23 in humans).', 11),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'What is a genetic bottleneck?', 'A sharp fall in population size that reduces genetic diversity, e.g. northern elephant seals.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'What is the founder effect?', 'A few individuals establish a new population, carrying only a small part of the original gene pool, which reduces genetic diversity.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'How does courtship behaviour help classification?', 'Courtship is species-specific, so it allows members of the same species to recognise each other and mate.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Genetic Information & Variation', 'What is phylogenetic classification?', 'Grouping organisms by their evolutionary relationships and common ancestry.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'What is the role of chlorophyll in photosynthesis?', 'It absorbs light energy, which excites electrons in photosystems, starting the light-dependent reaction.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'What is photolysis?', 'The splitting of water by light: 2H2O → 4H+ + 4e− + O2. It replaces electrons lost from chlorophyll.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'What are the products of the light-dependent reaction used for?', 'ATP and reduced NADP are used in the light-independent reaction (Calvin cycle) to reduce GP to triose phosphate.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'What is the role of RuBisCO?', 'It catalyses the fixation of CO2 with ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP) to form two molecules of glycerate-3-phosphate (GP).', 4),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'Why does the concentration of RuBP rise and GP fall when CO2 is limited?', 'Less RuBP is used to fix CO2, while RuBP is still regenerated from triose phosphate, and less GP is formed.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'Where do the light-dependent and light-independent reactions take place?', 'The light-dependent reaction takes place in the thylakoid membranes; the light-independent reaction takes place in the stroma.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'What is the net ATP yield from glycolysis per glucose?', '2 ATP (4 made minus 2 used), plus 2 reduced NAD and 2 pyruvate.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'What happens in the link reaction?', 'Pyruvate is decarboxylated and oxidised to acetate, which combines with coenzyme A to form acetyl coenzyme A; reduced NAD and CO2 are produced.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'What is the role of oxygen in respiration?', 'It is the final electron acceptor at the end of the electron transport chain, combining with electrons and protons to form water.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'Why is anaerobic respiration less efficient?', 'Only glycolysis produces ATP (2 per glucose); the link reaction, Krebs cycle and oxidative phosphorylation can''t occur without oxygen.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'What is the role of NAD and FAD?', 'Coenzymes that accept hydrogen (become reduced) in glycolysis, the link reaction and Krebs cycle, carrying it to the electron transport chain.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'What is primary productivity measured in?', 'Energy per unit area per unit time, e.g. kJ m^-2 yr^-1.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'How can farming practices increase efficiency of energy transfer?', 'Reducing respiratory losses (restricting movement, keeping animals warm), and removing competitors and pests.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'What is the role of mycorrhizae?', 'Fungi associated with plant roots that increase the surface area for water and mineral uptake; in return, they receive carbohydrates.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Energy Transfers', 'What is nitrogen fixation?', 'The conversion of atmospheric nitrogen gas into ammonium compounds by bacteria such as Rhizobium in root nodules.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Organisms Respond to Changes', 'What is a kinesis?', 'A non-directional response in which the rate of movement changes with the intensity of a stimulus, e.g. woodlice moving faster in dry conditions.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Organisms Respond to Changes', 'What is a taxis?', 'A directional movement towards (positive) or away from (negative) a stimulus.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Organisms Respond to Changes', 'How does IAA cause phototropism in shoots?', 'IAA moves to the shaded side, stimulating cell elongation there, so the shoot bends towards the light.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Organisms Respond to Changes', 'Why does IAA cause roots to grow downwards?', 'IAA builds up on the lower side of roots and inhibits cell elongation there, so the upper side grows more and the root bends downwards.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Organisms Respond to Changes', 'Why do cones give higher visual acuity than rods?', 'Each cone connects to its own bipolar neurone, so the brain receives separate impulses from each.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Organisms Respond to Changes', 'What are the roles of the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems in controlling heart rate?', 'Sympathetic increases the frequency of impulses from the SAN, speeding up heart rate. Parasympathetic decreases it, slowing heart rate.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Organisms Respond to Changes', 'What is the refractory period and why is it important?', 'After an action potential, the axon membrane can''t be stimulated again. This makes impulses discrete, unidirectional, and limits their frequency.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Organisms Respond to Changes', 'What factors affect speed of conduction of an impulse?', 'Myelination (saltatory conduction), axon diameter (wider is faster), and temperature.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Organisms Respond to Changes', 'What is a neuromuscular junction?', 'A synapse between a motor neurone and a muscle fibre, using acetylcholine to trigger muscle contraction.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Organisms Respond to Changes', 'What is the role of calcium ions in muscle contraction?', 'Ca2+ binds to troponin, moving tropomyosin to expose myosin binding sites on actin, so cross-bridges can form.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Organisms Respond to Changes', 'What is the role of ATP in muscle contraction?', 'ATP hydrolysis provides energy for the power stroke and allows myosin heads to detach from actin.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'What is codominance?', 'Both alleles are expressed in the phenotype of a heterozygote, e.g. the AB blood group.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'What are multiple alleles?', 'A gene with more than two alleles, e.g. IA, IB and IO for ABO blood groups.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'Why does a dihybrid cross with linked genes not give a 9:3:3:1 ratio?', 'Linked genes are on the same chromosome and usually inherited together, so fewer recombinant phenotypes appear (unless crossing over occurs).', 3),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'In the Hardy–Weinberg equations, what do p and q represent?', 'p is the frequency of the dominant allele and q the frequency of the recessive allele; p + q = 1.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'What conditions are required for Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium?', 'A large population, random mating, no mutation, no migration and no selection.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'What is genetic drift?', 'A random change in allele frequency, most significant in small populations.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'What is a gene pool?', 'All the alleles of all the genes in a population at a given time.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'What is a population?', 'All the organisms of one species living in the same area at the same time that can interbreed.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'What is a community?', 'All the populations of different species living in the same area at the same time.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'What is a niche?', 'The role of a species within its habitat, including its biotic and abiotic interactions.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'What is interspecific competition?', 'Competition between different species for the same resources, which can reduce population sizes or exclude one species.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'What is a pioneer species?', 'The first species to colonise bare ground in succession, e.g. lichens, which can survive in harsh abiotic conditions.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'What is a climax community?', 'The final stable community in succession, with high biodiversity.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'What conditions are needed for mark–release–recapture to be valid?', 'Marks don''t affect survival, marked individuals mix fully, and there is no significant migration, birth or death between samples.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T7', 'Genetics, Populations & Evolution', 'Why might chi-squared show a significant difference from an expected ratio?', 'Linkage, epistasis, sex linkage, lethal alleles, or a small sample size.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T8', 'Control of Gene Expression', 'What is a transcription factor?', 'A protein that binds to a specific DNA promoter region to stimulate or inhibit transcription of a gene.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T8', 'Control of Gene Expression', 'What is epigenetics?', 'Heritable changes in gene function without changes to the base sequence of DNA, e.g. methylation and histone acetylation.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T8', 'Control of Gene Expression', 'How does increased methylation affect transcription?', 'Methyl groups on the DNA (usually at CpG sites) prevent transcription factors from binding, silencing the gene.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T8', 'Control of Gene Expression', 'How does acetylation of histones affect transcription?', 'Acetylation loosens chromatin, allowing transcription factors to bind and increasing transcription.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T8', 'Control of Gene Expression', 'What is a benign tumour and a malignant tumour?', 'Benign: slow-growing, doesn''t spread, usually encapsulated. Malignant: grows rapidly, invades tissues and spreads (metastasis).', 5),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T8', 'Control of Gene Expression', 'How does oestrogen increase the risk of breast cancer?', 'It can activate transcription of genes that promote cell division, e.g. in cells with mutated genes, leading to tumour growth.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T8', 'Control of Gene Expression', 'What is the Human Genome Project and one benefit of it?', 'The mapping of the complete human DNA sequence. It helps identify disease-causing mutations and develop personalised medicine.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T8', 'Control of Gene Expression', 'Why is it difficult to translate the genome of complex organisms into a proteome?', 'They have non-coding DNA and regulatory genes, and alternative splicing produces many proteins from one gene.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T8', 'Control of Gene Expression', 'What are sticky ends?', 'Short single-stranded overhangs produced by restriction enzymes cutting at palindromic sequences, allowing complementary DNA fragments to join.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T8', 'Control of Gene Expression', 'How is reverse transcriptase used to make a gene?', 'It makes complementary DNA (cDNA) from mRNA, so the gene has no introns and can be expressed in bacteria.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T8', 'Control of Gene Expression', 'What is the role of DNA ligase?', 'It joins the sugar–phosphate backbones of DNA fragments by forming phosphodiester bonds.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T8', 'Control of Gene Expression', 'Give two uses of recombinant DNA technology.', 'Production of human insulin in bacteria, and GM crops (e.g. Golden Rice with added vitamin A).', 12),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T8', 'Control of Gene Expression', 'What are VNTRs and why are they useful in DNA profiling?', 'Variable number tandem repeats: non-coding repeated sequences that vary between individuals, so they give a unique profile.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'T8', 'Control of Gene Expression', 'How is genetic screening used?', 'Labelled DNA probes identify specific alleles, e.g. BRCA1 mutations or cystic fibrosis, to inform decisions and treatment.', 14)
),
fresh AS (
  SELECT v.* FROM v
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM flashcards f
    WHERE f.subject = v.subject AND f.exam_board = v.exam_board AND f.level = v.level
      AND f.topic_id = v.topic_id AND f.front = v.front)
),
cur AS (
  SELECT subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, count(*) AS n, max(card_order) AS mx
  FROM flashcards
  WHERE subject = 'Biology' AND exam_board = 'AQA' AND level = 'alevel'
  GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4
),
ranked AS (
  SELECT fr.*, coalesce(c.n, 0) AS n, coalesce(c.mx, 0) AS mx,
         row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY fr.topic_id ORDER BY fr.ord) AS rn
  FROM fresh fr
  LEFT JOIN cur c USING (subject, exam_board, level, topic_id)
)
INSERT INTO flashcards (subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, card_order, level)
SELECT subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, mx + rn, level
FROM ranked
WHERE n + rn <= 30;

-- Flashcard top-up: Biology|Edexcel A|alevel — brings each topic up to 30 cards.
-- Generated by scripts/build-flashcard-topup.js from flashcards/topup/src/biology-edexcel-a-alevel.txt
-- Safe to re-run: skips fronts already present and never takes a topic past 30.
WITH v(subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, ord) AS (VALUES
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'Why do larger animals need a mass transport system?', 'They have a small surface area to volume ratio and large diffusion distances, so diffusion alone is too slow to supply all cells.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'Describe the structure of an artery and relate it to function.', 'Thick muscular and elastic walls withstand and maintain high pressure, and recoil to smooth blood flow; the lumen is narrow.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'How does blood clotting occur?', 'Damaged tissue and platelets release thromboplastin, which converts prothrombin to thrombin (with Ca2+); thrombin converts soluble fibrinogen to insoluble fibrin, forming a mesh that traps cells.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'What are monosaccharides, disaccharides and polysaccharides?', 'Monosaccharides are single sugar units (glucose); disaccharides are two joined by a glycosidic bond (maltose); polysaccharides are many joined (starch, glycogen).', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'Describe the structure of amylose and amylopectin.', 'Amylose: unbranched chains of α-glucose with 1,4-glycosidic bonds, coiling into a helix. Amylopectin: branched, with 1,6-glycosidic bonds as well.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'How does glycogen differ from amylopectin?', 'Glycogen has more branches and shorter chains, so it can be hydrolysed faster to release glucose.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'How is a triglyceride formed?', 'By condensation of one glycerol and three fatty acids, forming three ester bonds and releasing three water molecules.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'What is the role of blood cholesterol in CVD?', 'High LDL levels deposit cholesterol in artery walls, contributing to atheroma formation.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'What is an energy budget?', 'The balance between energy intake (food) and energy output (BMR and activity). A positive budget leads to weight gain.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'How are BMI and waist-to-hip ratio used?', 'BMI = mass (kg)/height^2 (m^2); a waist-to-hip ratio above 0.9 (men) or 0.85 (women) suggests increased CVD risk from abdominal fat.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'How do antihypertensives reduce CVD risk?', 'They lower blood pressure (e.g. diuretics, beta blockers, ACE inhibitors), reducing endothelial damage.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'Give a risk of taking statins.', 'Side effects such as muscle pain, liver damage and digestive problems.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'How do anticoagulants and platelet inhibitors reduce CVD risk?', 'They reduce the formation of blood clots (e.g. warfarin, aspirin), but increase the risk of excessive bleeding.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'What is hypertension and how does it increase CVD risk?', 'Persistently high blood pressure, which damages the endothelium of arteries and starts atherosclerosis.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'Why are people''s perceptions of risk often inaccurate?', 'They overestimate unfamiliar or dramatic risks and underestimate familiar or voluntary ones (e.g. smoking).', 15),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'What is the role of a double-blind trial?', 'Neither participants nor researchers know who receives the treatment or placebo, reducing bias.', 16),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S1', 'Lifestyle, Health and Risk', 'Why is the heart''s left ventricle wall thicker than the right?', 'It pumps blood around the whole body at high pressure, while the right ventricle only pumps to the nearby lungs.', 17),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'What is the role of the quaternary structure of haemoglobin?', 'Four polypeptide chains (2α, 2β), each with a haem group, allow cooperative binding of four O2 molecules.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'What is a globular protein? Give an example.', 'A compact, spherical, soluble protein with a specific shape, e.g. haemoglobin or enzymes.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'What is a fibrous protein? Give an example.', 'A long, insoluble protein with a structural role, e.g. collagen, which forms strong fibres from three chains wound together.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'What is the role of mRNA?', 'It carries a copy of the genetic code from DNA in the nucleus to the ribosomes in the cytoplasm.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'Why does a mutation not always affect protein structure?', 'The code is degenerate, so a substitution may still code for the same amino acid, or the mutation may be in an intron.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'What is a gene?', 'A sequence of bases on DNA that codes for the amino acid sequence of a polypeptide.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'Explain how enzymes lower activation energy.', 'They form an enzyme–substrate complex, which puts strain on bonds or brings reactants together, making the reaction easier.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'How is the initial rate of an enzyme reaction calculated?', 'Draw a tangent to the curve at t = 0 and calculate its gradient.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'How does the CFTR protein function normally?', 'It is a channel protein that transports chloride ions out of epithelial cells, drawing water after them to keep the mucus thin.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'Why does cystic fibrosis affect the digestive system?', 'Thick mucus blocks the pancreatic duct, so digestive enzymes can''t reach the small intestine; food is poorly absorbed.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'Why does cystic fibrosis affect the reproductive system?', 'Thick mucus blocks the sperm ducts (vas deferens) in males and the cervix in females, reducing fertility.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'What is gene therapy for cystic fibrosis?', 'Inserting normal CFTR alleles into the epithelial cells of the lungs using viral vectors or liposomes.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'What is a carrier?', 'A heterozygous individual with one recessive allele for a condition, who doesn''t show symptoms but can pass it on.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'What is the difference between a genotype and a phenotype?', 'Genotype: the alleles an organism has. Phenotype: its observable characteristics, resulting from its genotype and environment.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S2', 'Genes and Health', 'What is preimplantation genetic diagnosis (PGD)?', 'Testing embryos produced by IVF for genetic disorders before implantation, so only unaffected embryos are implanted.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'What is the role of ribosomes?', 'The site of protein synthesis (translation), made of rRNA and protein.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'What is the role of rough endoplasmic reticulum?', 'It has ribosomes on its surface, and it transports and folds proteins made on them.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'How are proteins secreted from a cell?', 'Made on the RER, transported in vesicles to the Golgi to be modified and packaged, then carried in vesicles to fuse with the cell membrane (exocytosis).', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'What is the structure of a prokaryotic cell?', 'No nucleus or membrane-bound organelles; circular DNA, plasmids, 70S ribosomes, a cell wall, and sometimes a capsule, pili and flagella.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'What are the functions of the acrosome and zona pellucida?', 'The acrosome releases enzymes that digest the zona pellucida (the glycoprotein layer around the egg) so sperm can penetrate.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'What is the cortical reaction?', 'After a sperm enters, cortical granules release enzymes that harden the zona pellucida, preventing polyspermy.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'What happens in the S phase of interphase?', 'DNA is replicated.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'What is a chromatid?', 'One of two identical copies of a replicated chromosome, joined at the centromere.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'Why is mitosis important?', 'Growth, repair, asexual reproduction, and producing genetically identical cells.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'What is a homologous pair of chromosomes?', 'Two chromosomes (one maternal, one paternal) with the same genes at the same loci, but possibly different alleles.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'What happens in anaphase of mitosis?', 'Spindle fibres shorten, separating the chromatids at the centromere and pulling them to opposite poles.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'What is cell differentiation?', 'The process by which cells become specialised for a function, by expressing only certain genes.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'How can differential gene expression lead to specialisation?', 'Transcription factors activate or repress certain genes, so only specific proteins are produced in each cell type.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'What is polygenic inheritance?', 'A characteristic controlled by many genes, giving continuous variation, e.g. height.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'What is the role of the environment in phenotype? Give an example.', 'The environment can modify gene expression, e.g. nutrition affects height, and sunlight affects skin colour.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'What is epigenetics?', 'Changes in gene expression without changes to DNA sequence, e.g. DNA methylation or histone modification, which can be inherited.', 16),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S3', 'Voice of the Genome', 'How does cell division by meiosis produce haploid cells?', 'Two divisions: homologous chromosomes separate in meiosis I, then chromatids separate in meiosis II, giving four haploid cells.', 17),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'What is natural selection?', 'Individuals with advantageous alleles are more likely to survive and reproduce, passing on those alleles, so allele frequency increases over generations.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'What is an adaptation?', 'A feature that increases an organism''s chance of survival and reproduction in its environment.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'Distinguish behavioural, physiological and anatomical adaptations.', 'Behavioural: actions (migration). Physiological: internal processes (hibernation). Anatomical: structures (thick fur).', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'What is species richness?', 'The number of different species in an area.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'What is genetic diversity?', 'The variety of alleles in the gene pool of a species or population.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'How is heterozygosity index calculated?', 'H = number of heterozygotes ÷ number of individuals in the population.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'Describe the structure of cellulose.', 'Long, straight chains of β-glucose linked by 1,4-glycosidic bonds, with alternate glucose molecules inverted; hydrogen bonds link chains into microfibrils.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'What is the function of sclerenchyma fibres?', 'They provide mechanical support; they are dead cells with walls thickened with lignin.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'Why are plant fibres useful to humans?', 'They are strong and are used for ropes and fabrics, e.g. hemp and flax.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'How are starch and cellulose used in sustainable products?', 'Starch can make bioplastics, and cellulose fibres make biodegradable products.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'What minerals do plants need and why?', 'Nitrates (amino acids, nucleic acids), magnesium (chlorophyll), calcium (cell walls, calcium pectate) and phosphates (ATP, DNA).', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'What is the role of the plasmodesmata?', 'Channels through plant cell walls linking cytoplasm of neighbouring cells, allowing transport and communication.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'How can the antimicrobial properties of plants be investigated?', 'Place discs soaked in plant extracts on agar seeded with bacteria and measure the zones of inhibition, using aseptic technique.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'What is the role of the Millennium Seed Bank?', 'It stores seeds of many plant species at low temperature and humidity to conserve genetic diversity for future reintroduction.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'What are the problems with captive breeding?', 'Small populations have low genetic diversity, animals may not adapt to the wild, and it is expensive.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S4', 'Biodiversity and Natural Resources', 'How can reintroduction programmes succeed?', 'Habitat restoration, community involvement, monitoring, and gradual acclimatisation of animals.', 16),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'What is the role of ATP in photosynthesis?', 'It provides energy for the reduction of GP to GALP in the Calvin cycle and for regenerating RuBP.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'What is photophosphorylation?', 'The production of ATP from ADP and Pi using light energy, via an electron transport chain in the thylakoid membrane.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'What happens to GALP produced in the Calvin cycle?', 'Most is used to regenerate RuBP; some is used to make glucose, starch, cellulose, amino acids and lipids.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'What is the role of NADP in photosynthesis?', 'It accepts electrons and hydrogen ions in the light-dependent reaction (forming reduced NADP), which are used to reduce GP in the Calvin cycle.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'What is gross primary productivity?', 'The total rate at which energy is fixed as chemical energy by producers through photosynthesis.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'Why is energy lost between trophic levels?', 'Energy is lost through respiration (heat), excretion, egestion, and parts not eaten.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'How is efficiency of energy transfer calculated?', '(Energy in the higher trophic level ÷ energy in the lower trophic level) × 100%.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'What is the greenhouse effect?', 'Greenhouse gases absorb infrared radiation re-emitted from the Earth''s surface and re-radiate it, warming the atmosphere.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'Why are methane and CO2 levels rising?', 'CO2: burning fossil fuels and deforestation. Methane: cattle farming, rice paddies and landfill.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'How are dendrochronology and ice cores used as evidence for climate change?', 'Tree ring widths show past growing conditions; air bubbles in ice cores show past atmospheric CO2 and temperature.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'How does temperature affect enzyme-controlled processes in organisms?', 'Rates increase up to an optimum; above it, enzymes denature. The Q10 value describes the change for a 10 °C rise.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'How is Q10 calculated?', 'Q10 = rate at (T + 10 °C) ÷ rate at T.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'What is a biotic and an abiotic factor?', 'Biotic: living factors (predation, competition, disease). Abiotic: non-living factors (temperature, light, pH, water).', 13),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'What is a climax community?', 'The final, stable community in succession, e.g. oak woodland in the UK.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'How do scientists use evidence to reach a consensus on climate change?', 'Peer review of data and models, repeated measurements from different sources, and bodies such as the IPCC assessing all the evidence.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S5', 'On the Wild Side', 'What is the role of decomposers in the carbon cycle?', 'They break down dead organic matter, releasing CO2 through respiration.', 16),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'What is the structure of a bacterium and a virus?', 'Bacterium: prokaryotic cell with a cell wall, circular DNA, plasmids and ribosomes. Virus: nucleic acid in a protein capsid, sometimes with a lipid envelope.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'How does Mycobacterium tuberculosis cause disease?', 'It infects lung macrophages and survives inside them, forming tubercles; the lung tissue is damaged and the immune system is weakened.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'How does HIV affect the immune system?', 'It infects T helper cells, reducing their number, so the immune system can''t respond effectively, leading to AIDS and opportunistic infections.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'What is the lytic cycle of a virus?', 'The virus attaches, injects its nucleic acid, replicates using host cell machinery, assembles new viruses, and bursts (lyses) the cell.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'What is the latent (lysogenic) cycle?', 'Viral DNA integrates into the host genome and stays dormant, replicating with the host, until triggered to enter the lytic cycle.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'What are the barriers to infection?', 'Skin, stomach acid, mucous membranes, lysozyme in tears, and gut and skin microbiota.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'What is inflammation?', 'Mast cells release histamine, dilating blood vessels and increasing permeability, so phagocytes and plasma move into the tissue.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'What is the role of interferon?', 'Proteins released by virus-infected cells that inhibit viral replication in neighbouring cells.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'What are memory cells?', 'Long-lived B and T cells that remain after infection and respond rapidly to the same antigen on reinfection.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'What are bacteriostatic and bactericidal antibiotics?', 'Bacteriostatic antibiotics prevent bacterial growth (e.g. tetracycline); bactericidal antibiotics kill bacteria (e.g. penicillin).', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'How can hospitals reduce antibiotic resistance?', 'Prescribing antibiotics only when necessary, completing courses, hand hygiene, and screening and isolating infected patients.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'How does rigor mortis help estimate time of death?', 'Muscles stiffen 2–6 hours after death as ATP runs out and cross-bridges stay fixed; it passes after about 36 hours as proteins break down.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'How does body temperature help estimate time of death?', 'The body cools at about 1.5 °C per hour after death, depending on conditions.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'How does forensic entomology estimate time of death?', 'The species and developmental stage of insects (e.g. blowfly larvae) on the body indicate how long it has been dead.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'What is the role of gel electrophoresis in DNA profiling?', 'It separates DNA fragments by length: smaller fragments move faster through the gel towards the positive electrode.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S6', 'Immunity, Infection and Forensics', 'What is the evolutionary race between pathogens and host?', 'Pathogens evolve to evade the immune system (e.g. antigenic variation), while hosts evolve better defences.', 16),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'What is the role of coenzyme A?', 'It carries acetyl groups from the link reaction to the Krebs cycle.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'Where does glycolysis take place and what is produced?', 'In the cytoplasm: glucose is converted into 2 pyruvate, with a net gain of 2 ATP and 2 reduced NAD.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'What happens to lactate after anaerobic respiration?', 'It is carried to the liver and converted back to pyruvate (or glucose), using oxygen, which repays the oxygen debt.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'What is the role of the electron transport chain?', 'Electrons pass along carriers, releasing energy to pump H+ into the intermembrane space. H+ flows back through ATP synthase, making ATP.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'How do skeletal muscles and joints work together?', 'Muscles are attached to bones by tendons and work in antagonistic pairs across joints; ligaments hold bones together.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'What is the role of troponin and tropomyosin?', 'At rest, tropomyosin blocks the myosin binding sites on actin. Ca2+ binds to troponin, moving tropomyosin and exposing the sites.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'What are the adaptations of slow-twitch muscle fibres?', 'Many mitochondria, lots of myoglobin, and a rich capillary supply, suited to aerobic respiration and endurance.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'What does the SAN do?', 'The sinoatrial node is the pacemaker: it generates waves of excitation that spread across the atria.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'How does the cardiovascular control centre alter heart rate?', 'In the medulla, it receives input from chemoreceptors and baroreceptors, and sends impulses by sympathetic (increase) or vagus/parasympathetic (decrease) nerves.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'How does exercise affect breathing rate?', 'Increased CO2 lowers blood pH, detected by chemoreceptors; the ventilation centre increases the rate and depth of breathing.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'What is the role of the hypothalamus in thermoregulation?', 'It detects blood temperature and triggers responses: sweating and vasodilation to cool, or shivering and vasoconstriction to warm.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'What is negative feedback?', 'A change from the norm triggers a response that restores the norm.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'What is positive feedback?', 'A change triggers a response that increases the change further, e.g. oxytocin release during childbirth.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'How can excessive exercise damage the body?', 'Wear and tear on joints, and suppression of the immune system, increasing the risk of infection.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'How does erythropoietin (EPO) enhance performance?', 'It stimulates red blood cell production, increasing the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S7', 'Run for Your Life', 'What are the risks of taking anabolic steroids?', 'Liver damage, high blood pressure, infertility, and aggression.', 16),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'What is the structure of a sensory, relay and motor neurone?', 'Sensory: long dendron and short axon, cell body in the middle. Relay: short, many connections, in the CNS. Motor: cell body in the CNS, long axon.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'What is the function of the autonomic nervous system?', 'Controls involuntary actions (heart rate, digestion), with sympathetic (fight or flight) and parasympathetic (rest and digest) divisions.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'What is the role of the sodium–potassium pump?', 'It actively pumps 3 Na+ out of and 2 K+ into the axon, helping to maintain the resting potential.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'Describe depolarisation and repolarisation.', 'Depolarisation: voltage-gated Na+ channels open, Na+ floods in. Repolarisation: Na+ channels close and K+ channels open, K+ diffuses out.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'What is hyperpolarisation?', 'The potential becomes more negative than the resting potential because K+ channels are slow to close.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'What is the pupil reflex?', 'Bright light is detected by the retina; impulses go to the brain, and circular muscles of the iris contract, making the pupil smaller.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'How is light detected by rods?', 'Light bleaches rhodopsin into retinal and opsin, which closes Na+ channels, hyperpolarising the rod and stopping neurotransmitter release. This triggers an action potential in bipolar neurones.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'What is habituation?', 'A reduced response to a repeated harmless stimulus, caused by less neurotransmitter being released at synapses.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'What is the critical window for visual development?', 'A period in early life when the visual cortex must receive stimulation to develop normally, shown by Hubel and Wiesel''s kittens.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'What is the role of the hypothalamus?', 'It regulates body temperature, water balance and hunger, and controls the pituitary gland.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'How do fMRI scans work?', 'They detect changes in blood flow (oxygenated haemoglobin) to active brain areas, showing function.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'How does a drug like L-DOPA treat Parkinson''s disease?', 'It is converted to dopamine in the brain, replacing lost dopamine and reducing symptoms.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'What is depression linked to?', 'Low levels of serotonin. SSRIs block serotonin reuptake, increasing its level at synapses.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'What is the Human Genome Project''s role in developing drugs?', 'It helps identify target genes and proteins, and personalised medicine based on genotype (pharmacogenomics).', 14),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel A', 'alevel', 'S8', 'Grey Matter', 'How are GM organisms used to produce drugs?', 'Genes for human proteins are inserted into microorganisms, plants or animals, which produce the proteins (e.g. insulin by bacteria).', 15)
),
fresh AS (
  SELECT v.* FROM v
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM flashcards f
    WHERE f.subject = v.subject AND f.exam_board = v.exam_board AND f.level = v.level
      AND f.topic_id = v.topic_id AND f.front = v.front)
),
cur AS (
  SELECT subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, count(*) AS n, max(card_order) AS mx
  FROM flashcards
  WHERE subject = 'Biology' AND exam_board = 'Edexcel A' AND level = 'alevel'
  GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4
),
ranked AS (
  SELECT fr.*, coalesce(c.n, 0) AS n, coalesce(c.mx, 0) AS mx,
         row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY fr.topic_id ORDER BY fr.ord) AS rn
  FROM fresh fr
  LEFT JOIN cur c USING (subject, exam_board, level, topic_id)
)
INSERT INTO flashcards (subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, card_order, level)
SELECT subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, mx + rn, level
FROM ranked
WHERE n + rn <= 30;

-- Flashcard top-up: Biology|Edexcel B|alevel — brings each topic up to 30 cards.
-- Generated by scripts/build-flashcard-topup.js from flashcards/topup/src/biology-edexcel-b-alevel.txt
-- Safe to re-run: skips fronts already present and never takes a topic past 30.
WITH v(subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, ord) AS (VALUES
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '1', 'Biological Molecules', 'What is a glycosidic bond?', 'A covalent bond between two monosaccharides, formed by condensation with the loss of water.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '1', 'Biological Molecules', 'Why is glycogen more branched than amylopectin?', 'More branches give more ends for enzymes to act on, so glucose can be released quickly to meet animals'' high energy demands.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '1', 'Biological Molecules', 'What is an ester bond?', 'The bond formed between the –OH of glycerol and the –COOH of a fatty acid in a condensation reaction.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '1', 'Biological Molecules', 'Distinguish saturated and unsaturated fatty acids.', 'Saturated: no C=C double bonds, straight chains. Unsaturated: one or more C=C bonds, causing kinks in the chain.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '1', 'Biological Molecules', 'What is the primary structure of a protein?', 'The sequence of amino acids in a polypeptide chain, held by peptide bonds.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '1', 'Biological Molecules', 'What is the secondary structure of a protein?', 'Folding of the polypeptide into α-helices or β-pleated sheets, held by hydrogen bonds.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '1', 'Biological Molecules', 'What is the role of disulfide bridges?', 'Strong covalent bonds between sulfur atoms of cysteine R groups, stabilising the tertiary structure.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '1', 'Biological Molecules', 'Describe the structure of a DNA nucleotide.', 'A deoxyribose sugar, a phosphate group and a nitrogenous base (adenine, thymine, cytosine or guanine).', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '1', 'Biological Molecules', 'What is a phosphodiester bond?', 'The bond linking the phosphate of one nucleotide to the sugar of the next, forming the sugar–phosphate backbone.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '1', 'Biological Molecules', 'What is the role of mRNA in protein synthesis?', 'It carries a complementary copy of the gene''s base sequence from the nucleus to the ribosome.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '1', 'Biological Molecules', 'How does a non-competitive inhibitor work?', 'It binds to an allosteric site, changing the shape of the active site so the substrate no longer fits. Adding more substrate doesn''t reverse it.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '1', 'Biological Molecules', 'What is end-product inhibition?', 'The final product of a metabolic pathway inhibits an enzyme earlier in the pathway, regulating production.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '2', 'Cells, Viruses and Reproduction of Living Things', 'What is the role of ribosomes?', 'The site of translation (protein synthesis). 80S in eukaryotes, 70S in prokaryotes.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '2', 'Cells, Viruses and Reproduction of Living Things', 'What is the role of the cell wall in plants?', 'It provides support and strength and prevents the cell bursting when turgid; it is made of cellulose microfibrils.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '2', 'Cells, Viruses and Reproduction of Living Things', 'What is the role of the capsule in bacteria?', 'A slimy layer that protects against phagocytosis and desiccation, and helps bacteria stick to surfaces.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '2', 'Cells, Viruses and Reproduction of Living Things', 'What is the difference between Gram-positive and Gram-negative cell walls?', 'Gram-positive: thick peptidoglycan layer, stains purple. Gram-negative: thin peptidoglycan with an outer lipid membrane, stains red/pink.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '2', 'Cells, Viruses and Reproduction of Living Things', 'What is a bacteriophage?', 'A virus that infects bacteria.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '2', 'Cells, Viruses and Reproduction of Living Things', 'What is a retrovirus?', 'An RNA virus that uses reverse transcriptase to make DNA from its RNA, which is inserted into the host genome, e.g. HIV.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '2', 'Cells, Viruses and Reproduction of Living Things', 'What happens during prophase of mitosis?', 'Chromosomes condense and become visible, the nuclear envelope breaks down, and spindle fibres form.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '2', 'Cells, Viruses and Reproduction of Living Things', 'What happens during metaphase of mitosis?', 'Chromosomes line up at the equator and attach to spindle fibres by their centromeres.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '2', 'Cells, Viruses and Reproduction of Living Things', 'What happens during telophase of mitosis?', 'Chromatids reach the poles, nuclear envelopes reform and chromosomes uncoil. Cytokinesis then divides the cytoplasm.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '2', 'Cells, Viruses and Reproduction of Living Things', 'What is the mitotic index?', 'The proportion of cells in a sample undergoing mitosis: cells in mitosis ÷ total cells.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '2', 'Cells, Viruses and Reproduction of Living Things', 'What happens in the acrosome reaction?', 'When sperm meet the zona pellucida, the acrosome releases digestive enzymes that allow the sperm to penetrate it.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '2', 'Cells, Viruses and Reproduction of Living Things', 'What is the endosperm?', 'A triploid tissue formed when a male nucleus fuses with the two polar nuclei; it provides food for the embryo.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '3', 'Classification and Biodiversity', 'What is phylogeny?', 'The evolutionary history and relationships of organisms.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '3', 'Classification and Biodiversity', 'What is a cladogram?', 'A branching diagram showing evolutionary relationships based on shared derived characteristics.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '3', 'Classification and Biodiversity', 'Why do molecular data give better classification than physical features?', 'Physical features can be similar due to convergent evolution; DNA and protein sequences give more objective evidence of relatedness.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '3', 'Classification and Biodiversity', 'What is ring species?', 'Populations where neighbouring groups can interbreed, but those at the ends of the ring cannot, showing speciation in progress.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '3', 'Classification and Biodiversity', 'What are the domains Archaea, Bacteria and Eukarya?', 'Archaea: prokaryotes with unique membranes, often in extreme environments. Bacteria: prokaryotes with peptidoglycan cell walls. Eukarya: cells with a nucleus.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '3', 'Classification and Biodiversity', 'What is a behavioural adaptation?', 'A way an organism acts to increase survival, e.g. birds migrating.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '3', 'Classification and Biodiversity', 'What is a physiological adaptation?', 'An internal process that increases survival, e.g. hibernation or producing venom.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '3', 'Classification and Biodiversity', 'What is an anatomical adaptation?', 'A structural feature that increases survival, e.g. a thick blubber layer in whales.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '3', 'Classification and Biodiversity', 'What is speciation?', 'The formation of new species when populations become reproductively isolated and diverge genetically.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '3', 'Classification and Biodiversity', 'Give two types of reproductive isolation.', 'Prezygotic (e.g. different mating seasons or courtship behaviour) and postzygotic (e.g. hybrid sterility).', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '3', 'Classification and Biodiversity', 'What is endemism?', 'A species found only in one particular geographic location.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '3', 'Classification and Biodiversity', 'What is biodiversity?', 'The variety of living organisms: species diversity, genetic diversity and habitat diversity.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '4', 'Exchange and Transport', 'How does the structure of the alveolus maximise gas exchange?', 'Large surface area, thin walls (one cell thick), moist lining, good blood supply and ventilation maintain steep concentration gradients.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '4', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is the role of surfactant?', 'It reduces surface tension in the alveoli, preventing them collapsing during exhalation.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '4', 'Exchange and Transport', 'How does counter-current flow in fish gills work?', 'Blood and water flow in opposite directions, maintaining a concentration gradient along the whole gill lamella.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '4', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is the role of the sinoatrial node?', 'It generates electrical impulses that start each heartbeat, making the atria contract.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '4', 'Exchange and Transport', 'Why is there a delay at the atrioventricular node?', 'It gives the atria time to empty into the ventricles before the ventricles contract.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '4', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is the role of the Purkyne fibres?', 'They carry impulses from the bundle of His to the ventricle walls, so the ventricles contract from the apex upwards.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '4', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What happens to the oxygen dissociation curve during exercise?', 'It shifts to the right (the Bohr effect) as CO2 increases, so haemoglobin releases more oxygen to muscles.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '4', 'Exchange and Transport', 'Why does fetal haemoglobin have a higher affinity for oxygen?', 'It can load oxygen from the mother''s blood in the placenta, where the partial pressure of oxygen is low.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '4', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is transpiration?', 'The loss of water vapour from the leaves, mainly through the stomata.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '4', 'Exchange and Transport', 'How does cohesion–tension move water up the xylem?', 'Water evaporates from leaves, pulling water molecules up because hydrogen bonds hold them together (cohesion); they also stick to xylem walls (adhesion).', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '4', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is translocation?', 'The movement of organic substances such as sucrose through the phloem from sources to sinks.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '4', 'Exchange and Transport', 'How are companion cells adapted?', 'They have many mitochondria for active transport of sucrose into the sieve tubes.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '5', 'Energy for Biological Processes', 'What is the role of NAD in respiration?', 'It accepts hydrogen (becoming reduced NAD) and carries it to the electron transport chain.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '5', 'Energy for Biological Processes', 'What happens to pyruvate in the absence of oxygen in animals?', 'It is reduced to lactate by reduced NAD, regenerating NAD so glycolysis can continue.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '5', 'Energy for Biological Processes', 'What is substrate-level phosphorylation?', 'ATP produced directly from a phosphorylated intermediate transferring a phosphate to ADP, e.g. in glycolysis and the Krebs cycle.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '5', 'Energy for Biological Processes', 'What is the respiratory quotient?', 'RQ = CO2 produced ÷ O2 consumed. Carbohydrates give about 1.0, lipids about 0.7 and proteins about 0.9.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '5', 'Energy for Biological Processes', 'Why do mitochondria have folded inner membranes (cristae)?', 'To increase the surface area for the electron transport chain and ATP synthase.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '5', 'Energy for Biological Processes', 'What is photolysis of water?', 'The splitting of water by light energy into protons, electrons and oxygen.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '5', 'Energy for Biological Processes', 'What is the role of photosystems I and II?', 'They contain chlorophyll that absorbs light, exciting electrons. PSII feeds electrons to the chain; PSI re-excites them to reduce NADP.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '5', 'Energy for Biological Processes', 'What is the role of RuBisCO?', 'It catalyses the combination of CO2 with RuBP to form two molecules of GP.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '5', 'Energy for Biological Processes', 'Why does the rate of photosynthesis plateau at high light intensity?', 'Another factor, such as CO2 concentration or temperature, becomes limiting.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '5', 'Energy for Biological Processes', 'What happens in the thylakoid membranes?', 'The light-dependent reactions: light absorption, photolysis, electron transport, and ATP and reduced NADP production.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '5', 'Energy for Biological Processes', 'What happens in the stroma?', 'The Calvin cycle (light-independent reactions), where CO2 is fixed and reduced to make GALP.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '5', 'Energy for Biological Processes', 'What is the Hill reaction?', 'Isolated chloroplasts reduce an artificial electron acceptor (e.g. DCPIP, blue to colourless) in light, showing the light-dependent reaction.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '6', 'Microbiology and Pathogens', 'What is a nutrient medium?', 'A substance containing the nutrients needed for microorganisms to grow, e.g. nutrient agar or broth.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '6', 'Microbiology and Pathogens', 'What are the phases of a bacterial growth curve?', 'Lag, log (exponential), stationary and death (decline).', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '6', 'Microbiology and Pathogens', 'Why do bacteria grow slowly in the lag phase?', 'They are adapting to the new environment, synthesising enzymes and taking in water, before dividing.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '6', 'Microbiology and Pathogens', 'Why does growth stop in the stationary phase?', 'Nutrients run out and toxic waste builds up, so the birth rate equals the death rate.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '6', 'Microbiology and Pathogens', 'What is a haemocytometer used for?', 'Counting cells (total count) in a known volume under a microscope.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '6', 'Microbiology and Pathogens', 'How does turbidimetry measure bacterial growth?', 'A colorimeter measures cloudiness (absorbance) of a culture, which increases with the number of cells (both living and dead).', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '6', 'Microbiology and Pathogens', 'What is a pathogen?', 'A microorganism that causes disease.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '6', 'Microbiology and Pathogens', 'How does a vector transmit disease?', 'An organism (e.g. a mosquito) carries a pathogen from one host to another without being affected.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '6', 'Microbiology and Pathogens', 'What is the role of phagocytes in the immune response?', 'They engulf and digest pathogens and present antigens to T cells.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '6', 'Microbiology and Pathogens', 'What is antigen presentation?', 'Phagocytes display a pathogen''s antigens on their surface, activating T helper cells.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '6', 'Microbiology and Pathogens', 'What is the role of T helper cells?', 'They release cytokines that activate B cells, T killer cells and phagocytes.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '6', 'Microbiology and Pathogens', 'What do plasma cells do?', 'They secrete large amounts of antibodies specific to one antigen.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '7', 'Modern Genetics', 'What is a DNA probe?', 'A short, single strand of labelled DNA complementary to a target sequence, used to find specific genes.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '7', 'Modern Genetics', 'What is gel electrophoresis?', 'A method that separates DNA fragments by size using an electric current; smaller fragments move further towards the positive electrode.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '7', 'Modern Genetics', 'What is the role of primers in PCR?', 'Short DNA sequences complementary to the start of the target region, providing a starting point for DNA polymerase.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '7', 'Modern Genetics', 'Why is PCR carried out at 95 °C, 55 °C and 72 °C?', '95 °C separates the strands; 55 °C lets primers anneal; 72 °C is the optimum for Taq polymerase to extend.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '7', 'Modern Genetics', 'What is a promoter region?', 'A DNA sequence upstream of a gene where RNA polymerase and transcription factors bind to start transcription.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '7', 'Modern Genetics', 'How can genetic modification improve crop yields?', 'Introducing genes for pest resistance, herbicide tolerance or drought resistance.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '7', 'Modern Genetics', 'What are the ethical concerns about GMOs?', 'Possible effects on the environment (gene transfer to wild plants), effects on biodiversity, corporate control of seeds, and long-term health effects.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '7', 'Modern Genetics', 'What is a vector in genetic engineering?', 'A carrier, such as a plasmid or virus, used to transfer DNA into a host cell.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '7', 'Modern Genetics', 'What is the role of marker genes?', 'They identify cells that have taken up recombinant DNA, e.g. antibiotic resistance or fluorescence genes.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '7', 'Modern Genetics', 'What are stem cells used for in medicine?', 'Replacing damaged cells or tissues, e.g. treating Parkinson''s disease, spinal cord injury and type 1 diabetes.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '7', 'Modern Genetics', 'Why are embryonic stem cells controversial?', 'Obtaining them destroys an embryo, which some people consider a potential human life.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '7', 'Modern Genetics', 'What is a transgenic organism?', 'An organism containing a gene from a different species.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '8', 'Origins of Genetic Variation', 'What is a mutation?', 'A change in the base sequence of DNA.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '8', 'Origins of Genetic Variation', 'What is a point mutation?', 'A change in a single base: a substitution, insertion or deletion.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '8', 'Origins of Genetic Variation', 'What is a chromosome mutation?', 'A change in the structure or number of chromosomes, e.g. deletion, duplication or translocation.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '8', 'Origins of Genetic Variation', 'What is dominant and recessive?', 'Dominant: an allele always expressed in the phenotype if present. Recessive: expressed only when two copies are present.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '8', 'Origins of Genetic Variation', 'What is a heterozygote advantage?', 'When heterozygotes have a survival advantage, e.g. sickle cell carriers are more resistant to malaria.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '8', 'Origins of Genetic Variation', 'What is epistasis?', 'When one gene affects or masks the expression of another gene.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '8', 'Origins of Genetic Variation', 'What is sex determination in humans?', 'Females are XX, males are XY. The SRY gene on the Y chromosome triggers male development.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '8', 'Origins of Genetic Variation', 'Why are males more likely to show X-linked recessive conditions?', 'They have only one X chromosome, so a single recessive allele is expressed.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '8', 'Origins of Genetic Variation', 'What is directional selection?', 'Selection that favours one extreme phenotype, shifting the mean, e.g. antibiotic resistance.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '8', 'Origins of Genetic Variation', 'What is allele frequency?', 'The proportion of a particular allele in a population''s gene pool.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '8', 'Origins of Genetic Variation', 'How does genetic drift differ from natural selection?', 'Genetic drift is random and not related to fitness; natural selection is non-random, favouring advantageous alleles.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '8', 'Origins of Genetic Variation', 'What are the Hardy–Weinberg equations?', 'p + q = 1 and p^2 + 2pq + q^2 = 1.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '9', 'Control Systems', 'What are the components of a control system?', 'A receptor, a coordinator (e.g. the brain) and an effector.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '9', 'Control Systems', 'What is the role of ADH?', 'Released from the posterior pituitary when blood water potential is low; it increases the permeability of the collecting duct to water, so more water is reabsorbed.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '9', 'Control Systems', 'What is ultrafiltration in the kidney?', 'High blood pressure in the glomerulus forces small molecules (water, glucose, urea, ions) through the capillary walls into the Bowman''s capsule.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '9', 'Control Systems', 'What is selective reabsorption?', 'The reabsorption of useful substances, such as glucose, amino acids and some water, back into the blood from the proximal convoluted tubule.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '9', 'Control Systems', 'Why is the loop of Henle longer in desert animals?', 'A longer loop creates a steeper water potential gradient in the medulla, so more water is reabsorbed and urine is more concentrated.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '9', 'Control Systems', 'What are the roles of the pancreas in blood glucose control?', 'β cells release insulin when glucose is high; α cells release glucagon when glucose is low.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '9', 'Control Systems', 'What is the difference between the nervous and endocrine systems?', 'Nervous: fast, short-lived, localised electrical impulses. Endocrine: slower, longer-lasting, widespread chemical hormones.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '9', 'Control Systems', 'What is a steroid hormone and how does it act?', 'A lipid-soluble hormone that diffuses into the cell and binds to receptors, acting as a transcription factor.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '9', 'Control Systems', 'What is the role of the fovea?', 'The part of the retina with many cones and no rods, giving the highest visual acuity.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '9', 'Control Systems', 'What is a reflex arc?', 'A rapid, involuntary response: receptor → sensory neurone → relay neurone → motor neurone → effector.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '9', 'Control Systems', 'What is the role of the hypothalamus in temperature control?', 'It detects changes in blood temperature and coordinates responses such as sweating, shivering and changes in blood flow to the skin.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '9', 'Control Systems', 'What is the role of ethylene in plants?', 'A plant hormone that promotes fruit ripening and leaf abscission.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '10', 'Ecosystems', 'What is a food web?', 'A diagram showing the interconnected feeding relationships between organisms in an ecosystem.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '10', 'Ecosystems', 'What is productivity?', 'The rate at which energy is converted into biomass, per unit area per unit time.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '10', 'Ecosystems', 'What is the role of decomposers?', 'They break down dead organic matter and waste, releasing nutrients back into the ecosystem.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '10', 'Ecosystems', 'What is nitrification?', 'The conversion of ammonium into nitrites and then nitrates by nitrifying bacteria (Nitrosomonas and Nitrobacter).', 4),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '10', 'Ecosystems', 'What is denitrification?', 'Anaerobic bacteria convert nitrates back into nitrogen gas.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '10', 'Ecosystems', 'What is the carbon cycle?', 'The movement of carbon through photosynthesis, respiration, feeding, decomposition, fossilisation and combustion.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '10', 'Ecosystems', 'How does deforestation affect the carbon cycle?', 'It reduces CO2 uptake by photosynthesis, and burning or decaying trees releases CO2.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '10', 'Ecosystems', 'What is a density-dependent factor?', 'A factor whose effect depends on population density, e.g. disease, competition and predation.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '10', 'Ecosystems', 'What is a density-independent factor?', 'A factor whose effect doesn''t depend on population density, e.g. floods, fire and temperature.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '10', 'Ecosystems', 'What is intraspecific competition?', 'Competition between members of the same species for resources.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '10', 'Ecosystems', 'What is the Lincoln index?', 'The population estimate from mark–recapture: N = (number marked first × total caught second) ÷ number marked in second sample.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'Edexcel B', 'alevel', '10', 'Ecosystems', 'What is conservation?', 'The protection and management of species and habitats to maintain biodiversity.', 12)
),
fresh AS (
  SELECT v.* FROM v
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM flashcards f
    WHERE f.subject = v.subject AND f.exam_board = v.exam_board AND f.level = v.level
      AND f.topic_id = v.topic_id AND f.front = v.front)
),
cur AS (
  SELECT subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, count(*) AS n, max(card_order) AS mx
  FROM flashcards
  WHERE subject = 'Biology' AND exam_board = 'Edexcel B' AND level = 'alevel'
  GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4
),
ranked AS (
  SELECT fr.*, coalesce(c.n, 0) AS n, coalesce(c.mx, 0) AS mx,
         row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY fr.topic_id ORDER BY fr.ord) AS rn
  FROM fresh fr
  LEFT JOIN cur c USING (subject, exam_board, level, topic_id)
)
INSERT INTO flashcards (subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, card_order, level)
SELECT subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, mx + rn, level
FROM ranked
WHERE n + rn <= 30;

-- Flashcard top-up: Biology|OCR A|alevel — brings each topic up to 30 cards.
-- Generated by scripts/build-flashcard-topup.js from flashcards/topup/src/biology-ocr-a-alevel.txt
-- Safe to re-run: skips fronts already present and never takes a topic past 30.
WITH v(subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, ord) AS (VALUES
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is a hypothesis?', 'A testable prediction or explanation, often stating the expected effect of the independent variable on the dependent variable.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is a null hypothesis?', 'A statement that there is no significant difference or relationship; statistical tests determine whether it can be rejected.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What does a p-value of 0.05 mean?', 'There is a 5% probability that the result is due to chance. If the test statistic exceeds the critical value at p = 0.05, the null hypothesis is rejected.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'How are degrees of freedom calculated for a chi-squared test?', 'Number of categories − 1.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'When is a Student''s t-test used?', 'To compare the means of two sets of continuous data that are normally distributed.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'When is Spearman''s rank correlation used?', 'To test for a correlation between two variables with at least 5 (ideally 10+) pairs of data.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What does a correlation coefficient of −1, 0 and +1 mean?', '−1: perfect negative correlation. 0: no correlation. +1: perfect positive correlation.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'Why doesn''t correlation prove causation?', 'Another variable could cause both changes, or the link may be coincidental; a controlled experiment is needed to show cause.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is the purpose of a control experiment?', 'It shows that the results are due to the independent variable and not other factors, e.g. using boiled enzyme.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'How is percentage error calculated for an instrument?', '(Uncertainty ÷ measured value) × 100.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'How is a biological drawing made correctly?', 'Sharp pencil, clear continuous lines, no shading, labels with ruled lines, a title and magnification, and drawing only what is seen.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is a stage micrometer used for?', 'Calibrating the eyepiece graticule at each magnification, so cells can be measured accurately.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is the formula for magnification?', 'Magnification = image size ÷ actual size.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is a pilot study?', 'A small preliminary investigation to test methods, find suitable ranges and identify problems before the main experiment.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'How can the reliability of results be improved?', 'Repeat readings, calculate means, identify and exclude anomalies, and use more precise equipment.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'What is the role of the cytoskeleton?', 'A network of protein fibres (microfilaments and microtubules) that supports the cell, moves organelles and vesicles, and allows cell movement.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'What is the role of centrioles?', 'Hollow cylinders of microtubules involved in forming the spindle during cell division (in animal cells).', 2),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'What is the role of lysosomes?', 'Vesicles containing hydrolytic enzymes that break down waste, worn-out organelles and pathogens.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'What is the role of the vacuole in plant cells?', 'It stores cell sap, maintains turgor pressure to support the cell, and is surrounded by the tonoplast.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'What is the Benedict''s test for reducing sugars?', 'Heat with Benedict''s reagent; the colour changes from blue to green, yellow, orange or brick-red depending on concentration.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'What is the biuret test for proteins?', 'Add biuret reagent; the colour changes from blue to lilac/purple if protein is present.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'What is the emulsion test for lipids?', 'Shake with ethanol, then add water; a white emulsion forms if lipid is present.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'What is the role of inorganic ions such as Ca2+ and NH4+?', 'Ca2+: nerve transmission, muscle contraction, bone. NH4+: nitrogen for amino acid production.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'What is a cofactor?', 'A non-protein substance needed for an enzyme to work, e.g. Cl− for amylase. A coenzyme is an organic cofactor (e.g. NAD).', 9),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'What is a prosthetic group?', 'A cofactor permanently bound to an enzyme, e.g. Zn2+ in carbonic anhydrase.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'What is the effect of enzyme concentration on the rate of reaction?', 'Rate increases in proportion to enzyme concentration, provided substrate is in excess.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'What is exocytosis?', 'Vesicles fuse with the cell surface membrane to release substances out of the cell, requiring ATP.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'What is endocytosis?', 'The membrane surrounds a substance and pinches off to form a vesicle inside the cell, requiring ATP.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'What are the roles of the cell surface membrane?', 'It controls what enters and leaves, and is involved in cell signalling, recognition and attachment.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Biology', 'How are cells organised into tissues, organs and systems?', 'Similar cells form tissues, tissues form organs, and organs work together as organ systems.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is the role of the intercostal muscles and diaphragm in breathing?', 'Inhalation: external intercostals and the diaphragm contract, increasing thorax volume. Exhalation: they relax (forced exhalation uses internal intercostals).', 1),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is vital capacity?', 'The maximum volume of air that can be breathed out after taking the deepest possible breath.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'How is a spirometer used?', 'A person breathes in and out of an oxygen-filled chamber; the trace shows tidal volume, vital capacity, breathing rate and oxygen uptake.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is an open circulatory system?', 'Blood is pumped into a body cavity and bathes the organs directly, e.g. in insects.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is a closed double circulatory system?', 'Blood is contained in vessels and passes through the heart twice per circuit (pulmonary and systemic), e.g. in mammals.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is the role of the chordae tendineae?', 'They attach the AV valves to the ventricle walls and prevent the valves inverting when the ventricles contract.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What does an ECG show?', 'The heart''s electrical activity: P wave (atrial depolarisation), QRS complex (ventricular depolarisation) and T wave (ventricular repolarisation).', 7),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is tachycardia and bradycardia?', 'Tachycardia: a resting heart rate over 100 bpm. Bradycardia: a resting heart rate under 60 bpm.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is the chloride shift?', 'HCO3− diffuses out of red blood cells into the plasma, and Cl− moves in to maintain electrical balance.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is the role of carbonic anhydrase?', 'An enzyme in red blood cells that catalyses CO2 + H2O → H2CO3, which dissociates into H+ and HCO3−.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is the apoplast pathway?', 'Water moves through cell walls and the spaces between cells until blocked by the Casparian strip.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is the symplast pathway?', 'Water moves through the cytoplasm of cells, via plasmodesmata.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What is the role of the Casparian strip?', 'A waterproof band of suberin in the endodermis that forces water into the symplast pathway, controlling what enters the xylem.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'What are the adaptations of hydrophytes?', 'Stomata on the upper surface, air spaces (aerenchyma) for buoyancy, thin cuticle and reduced xylem.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Exchange and Transport', 'How do environmental factors affect the rate of transpiration?', 'It increases with light intensity, temperature and air movement, and decreases with humidity.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'What is a communicable disease?', 'A disease caused by a pathogen that can be passed from one organism to another.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'Give an example of a bacterial disease in plants and animals.', 'Plants: ring rot (potatoes). Animals: tuberculosis and bacterial meningitis.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'Give examples of viral diseases in plants and animals.', 'Plants: tobacco mosaic virus. Animals: HIV/AIDS and influenza.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'Give examples of protoctist and fungal diseases.', 'Protoctist: malaria, potato blight. Fungal: athlete''s foot, black sigatoka (bananas), ringworm.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'What is the role of callose in plant defence?', 'A polysaccharide deposited in cell walls and sieve plates to block the spread of pathogens.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'What is an opsonin?', 'A molecule (e.g. an antibody) that binds to a pathogen and makes it easier for phagocytes to engulf.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'What is an autoimmune disease? Give an example.', 'The immune system attacks the body''s own cells, e.g. rheumatoid arthritis, type 1 diabetes and lupus.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'What are the sources of new medicines?', 'Natural sources (plants, microorganisms, e.g. penicillin), synthetic chemistry, and personalised medicine from genomics.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'What are the three types of variation?', 'Genetic (alleles), environmental and a combination of both. Variation can be continuous or discontinuous.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'What is a phylogenetic tree?', 'A diagram showing the evolutionary relationships between organisms and their common ancestors.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'What evidence is used to classify organisms today?', 'DNA and protein sequences, embryology and anatomy, and the fossil record.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'Give two examples of evolution through natural selection.', 'Antibiotic resistance in bacteria and pesticide resistance in insects (also peppered moths).', 12),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'What is the evidence for evolution?', 'Fossils, comparative anatomy (homologous structures) and comparative biochemistry (DNA and protein sequences).', 13),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'How does human activity affect biodiversity?', 'Habitat destruction, pollution, overexploitation, climate change and introducing non-native species.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Biodiversity, Evolution and Disease', 'What are the reasons for maintaining biodiversity?', 'Ecological (stability), economic (medicines, food, tourism) and aesthetic or ethical reasons.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Communication, Homeostasis and Energy', 'What are the roles of the endocrine glands?', 'They secrete hormones directly into the blood to act on target cells with complementary receptors.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Communication, Homeostasis and Energy', 'What is the structure of the adrenal gland?', 'The cortex secretes steroid hormones (e.g. cortisol, aldosterone); the medulla secretes adrenaline and noradrenaline.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Communication, Homeostasis and Energy', 'How does adrenaline act on cells?', 'It binds to receptors, activating adenylyl cyclase to make cyclic AMP (second messenger), which activates enzymes.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Communication, Homeostasis and Energy', 'How do the islets of Langerhans control blood glucose?', 'α cells secrete glucagon (raising glucose); β cells secrete insulin (lowering glucose).', 4),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Communication, Homeostasis and Energy', 'How is insulin secretion triggered?', 'High glucose enters β cells and increases ATP, which closes K+ channels, depolarising the cell. Ca2+ enters, triggering exocytosis of insulin.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Communication, Homeostasis and Energy', 'How do ectotherms control body temperature?', 'By behaviour, e.g. basking in the sun, seeking shade, and changing body orientation.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Communication, Homeostasis and Energy', 'What is the role of the liver in homeostasis?', 'It stores glycogen, deaminates excess amino acids (forming urea), detoxifies alcohol and drugs, and produces bile.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Communication, Homeostasis and Energy', 'What is kidney failure and how is it treated?', 'The kidneys can''t filter the blood effectively. Treatment is dialysis or a kidney transplant.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Communication, Homeostasis and Energy', 'What does a urine test for pregnancy detect?', 'hCG hormone, using monoclonal antibodies.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Communication, Homeostasis and Energy', 'What are the plant responses to abiotic stress?', 'Leaf loss (abscission) in cold, stomatal closure (abscisic acid), and production of antifreeze chemicals.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Communication, Homeostasis and Energy', 'What is the role of gibberellins?', 'They stimulate seed germination and stem elongation.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Communication, Homeostasis and Energy', 'What is the role of the cerebellum?', 'It coordinates muscle movement, balance and posture.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Communication, Homeostasis and Energy', 'What is the role of the medulla oblongata?', 'It controls involuntary processes such as heart rate, breathing rate and blood pressure.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Communication, Homeostasis and Energy', 'How is the role of chemiosmosis similar in chloroplasts and mitochondria?', 'In both, an electron transport chain pumps protons across a membrane, and they flow back through ATP synthase to make ATP.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What are the types of mutation and their effects?', 'Substitution (may be silent, missense or nonsense), insertion and deletion (frameshift). Effects can be neutral, harmful or beneficial.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What is a homeobox gene?', 'A gene containing a highly conserved 180-base-pair sequence that codes for transcription factors controlling body plan development.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What is apoptosis?', 'Programmed cell death, which removes unwanted cells during development and damaged cells.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What is continuous variation?', 'Variation with a range of values and no distinct categories, controlled by many genes and the environment, e.g. height.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What is discontinuous variation?', 'Variation in distinct categories, controlled by one or a few genes, e.g. blood group.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What is linkage?', 'Genes on the same chromosome that tend to be inherited together unless separated by crossing over.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What is a genetic bottleneck?', 'A large fall in population size that reduces the gene pool, so genetic diversity is lower in the new population.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What is artificial selection?', 'Humans choose individuals with desirable traits to breed, increasing those traits over generations, e.g. dairy cattle.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What is the role of DNA sequencing?', 'Determining the order of bases in DNA, used to identify genes, mutations and evolutionary relationships.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What is genetic engineering used for in agriculture?', 'Creating pest-resistant crops (Bt maize), herbicide-resistant crops and nutritionally enhanced crops (Golden Rice).', 10),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What is micropropagation?', 'Growing many identical plants from small pieces of tissue (explants) on a sterile medium containing hormones.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What is reproductive cloning in animals?', 'Somatic cell nuclear transfer: a nucleus from a body cell is placed into an enucleated egg, which develops into a clone (e.g. Dolly the sheep).', 12),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What is biotechnology?', 'The use of living organisms or their parts in industrial processes, e.g. brewing, baking, penicillin and enzyme production.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What is sustainable management?', 'Using resources in a way that meets current needs without preventing future generations from meeting theirs, e.g. coppicing or fishing quotas.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Genetics, Evolution and Ecosystems', 'What is the Galapagos Islands example of human impact?', 'Introduced species (goats, rats) and tourism have threatened native species; conservation projects remove invasive species and protect habitats.', 15)
),
fresh AS (
  SELECT v.* FROM v
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM flashcards f
    WHERE f.subject = v.subject AND f.exam_board = v.exam_board AND f.level = v.level
      AND f.topic_id = v.topic_id AND f.front = v.front)
),
cur AS (
  SELECT subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, count(*) AS n, max(card_order) AS mx
  FROM flashcards
  WHERE subject = 'Biology' AND exam_board = 'OCR A' AND level = 'alevel'
  GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4
),
ranked AS (
  SELECT fr.*, coalesce(c.n, 0) AS n, coalesce(c.mx, 0) AS mx,
         row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY fr.topic_id ORDER BY fr.ord) AS rn
  FROM fresh fr
  LEFT JOIN cur c USING (subject, exam_board, level, topic_id)
)
INSERT INTO flashcards (subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, card_order, level)
SELECT subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, mx + rn, level
FROM ranked
WHERE n + rn <= 30;

-- Flashcard top-up: Biology|OCR B|alevel — brings each topic up to 30 cards.
-- Generated by scripts/build-flashcard-topup.js from flashcards/topup/src/biology-ocr-b-alevel.txt
-- Safe to re-run: skips fronts already present and never takes a topic past 30.
WITH v(subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, ord) AS (VALUES
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is a hypothesis?', 'A testable statement predicting the relationship between variables, based on scientific knowledge.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is the null hypothesis in a statistical test?', 'The assumption that there is no significant difference or correlation; it is rejected if the test statistic exceeds the critical value.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is the significance level usually used in biology?', 'p = 0.05, meaning a 5% probability that the result occurred by chance.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'When would you use a Mann–Whitney U test?', 'To compare two sets of data that are not normally distributed (non-parametric).', 4),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is a paired t-test?', 'A t-test comparing two sets of measurements from the same individuals, e.g. before and after a treatment.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What does a standard deviation tell you?', 'The spread of data around the mean; a larger SD means more variation in the data.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is the purpose of error bars?', 'To show variability or uncertainty (e.g. ±1 SD or 95% confidence intervals); non-overlapping bars suggest a real difference.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is a confounding variable?', 'A variable other than the independent variable that could affect the dependent variable, making results unreliable.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'Why is random sampling important?', 'It avoids bias, so the sample is representative of the population.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'How can the precision of a measurement be improved?', 'Use instruments with a smaller resolution and repeat measurements.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'How should a line graph be plotted?', 'The independent variable on the x-axis and dependent on the y-axis, with suitable scales, labelled axes with units, and points joined or a best-fit line.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'When should a histogram be used?', 'For continuous data grouped into classes, where the bars touch.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is qualitative data?', 'Descriptive, non-numerical data, e.g. colour changes.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is quantitative data?', 'Numerical data that can be measured, e.g. mass, time or volume.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is peer review?', 'Other experts evaluate a scientific paper before publication to check the methods, results and conclusions are valid.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'What is the role of the nucleolus?', 'It makes ribosomal RNA and assembles ribosomes.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'What is the role of chloroplasts?', 'The site of photosynthesis; they contain chlorophyll in thylakoid membranes.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'How are cells specialised for their function? Give an example.', 'Red blood cells have no nucleus and a biconcave shape for more haemoglobin and a large surface area.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'What is the structure of haemoglobin?', 'A globular protein of four polypeptide chains (quaternary structure), each with a haem group containing Fe2+ that binds O2.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'What is the role of collagen?', 'A fibrous protein that provides strength in tendons, skin and bone, made of three polypeptide chains wound into a triple helix.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'What is the difference between α-glucose and β-glucose?', 'In α-glucose the –OH on carbon 1 is below the ring; in β-glucose it is above.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'What is the role of phospholipids?', 'They form the bilayer of cell membranes: hydrophilic phosphate heads face outwards and hydrophobic fatty acid tails face inwards.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'What factors affect the permeability of a membrane?', 'Temperature, solvents such as ethanol, and pH, which can disrupt phospholipids and denature proteins.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'What is the effect of placing an animal cell in pure water?', 'Water enters by osmosis and the cell swells and may burst (lysis) because there is no cell wall.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'What is plasmolysis?', 'Water leaves a plant cell in a solution with lower water potential, and the cytoplasm and membrane pull away from the cell wall.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'What is the difference between a closed and an open circulatory system?', 'Closed: blood stays in vessels (mammals). Open: blood bathes the tissues directly in a body cavity (insects).', 11),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'What is the cardiac cycle?', 'The sequence of events in one heartbeat: atrial systole, ventricular systole and diastole.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'What is the role of the lymphatic system?', 'It drains excess tissue fluid (lymph) back into the blood, and lymph nodes contain lymphocytes.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'What is the function of red blood cells?', 'They carry oxygen, bound to haemoglobin, from the lungs to the tissues.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B2', 'Cells, Chemicals and Transport', 'Why is ventilation needed in mammals?', 'To maintain a steep concentration gradient of O2 and CO2 between the alveoli and blood.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'What is a checkpoint in the cell cycle?', 'A control point where the cell checks conditions (e.g. DNA damage, correct replication) before moving to the next phase.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'What is the role of p53?', 'A tumour suppressor protein that stops the cell cycle if DNA is damaged and can trigger apoptosis.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'What is cytokinesis?', 'The division of the cytoplasm to form two daughter cells.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'What happens in meiosis I?', 'Homologous chromosomes pair up (crossing over can occur) and are separated into two cells.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'What happens in meiosis II?', 'Sister chromatids separate, producing four haploid cells.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'What is the role of stem cells in plants?', 'Meristems contain stem cells that divide by mitosis to produce new cells for growth.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'What are the uses of stem cells in medicine?', 'Treating leukaemia (bone marrow transplants), and potential treatments for spinal injuries, heart disease and diabetes.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'How does smoking increase cancer risk?', 'Carcinogens in tar cause mutations in oncogenes and tumour suppressor genes, leading to uncontrolled cell division.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'What is the role of the skin as a barrier?', 'The keratinised outer layer prevents pathogen entry, and sebum inhibits microbial growth.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'What are the roles of B cells and T cells?', 'B cells produce antibodies (humoral response). T cells activate other cells (helper) or kill infected cells (killer).', 10),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'What is clonal selection?', 'The specific B or T cell with complementary receptors binds to an antigen and divides by mitosis to form a clone.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'How does a vaccine work?', 'It contains antigens (weakened or dead pathogens or fragments) that stimulate a primary response and memory cells, without causing the disease.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'What is the role of mosquito nets and insecticides against malaria?', 'They prevent mosquitoes, the vectors of Plasmodium, from biting humans and transmitting the parasite.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'How does Plasmodium cause malaria?', 'The parasite infects liver cells, then red blood cells, where it multiplies and bursts them, causing fever and anaemia.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B3', 'Cell Division, Development and Disease', 'What is antibiotic resistance?', 'A mutation gives bacteria resistance; they survive antibiotic treatment and reproduce, so the resistant allele increases in the population.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'What are the products of the light-dependent reactions?', 'ATP, reduced NADP and oxygen (a by-product).', 1),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'What is the role of chlorophyll a and accessory pigments?', 'Chlorophyll a is the primary pigment at the reaction centre. Accessory pigments (chlorophyll b, carotenoids) absorb other wavelengths and pass energy to it.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'What is the limiting factor principle?', 'The rate of a process is limited by the factor in shortest supply.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'What is the role of the Krebs cycle?', 'It oxidises acetyl coenzyme A, producing CO2, reduced NAD, reduced FAD and ATP.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'What is the total ATP yield of aerobic respiration?', 'About 30–32 ATP per glucose molecule.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'What is the role of ATP synthase?', 'An enzyme in the inner mitochondrial membrane (and thylakoid) that uses the flow of protons to phosphorylate ADP to ATP.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'What is the difference between asexual and sexual reproduction?', 'Asexual: one parent, genetically identical offspring, no gametes. Sexual: two parents, gametes fuse, offspring are genetically varied.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'What is spermatogenesis?', 'The production of sperm in the seminiferous tubules of the testes by mitosis, meiosis and differentiation.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'What is the role of FSH and LH in the menstrual cycle?', 'FSH stimulates follicle development and oestrogen production. The LH surge triggers ovulation and corpus luteum formation.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'What is the role of progesterone?', 'It maintains the uterine lining (endometrium); falling levels cause menstruation.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'How is pollination different from fertilisation in plants?', 'Pollination is the transfer of pollen to a stigma; fertilisation is the fusion of male and female gametes.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'What is a population growth curve?', 'An S-shaped (sigmoid) curve with lag, exponential (log) and stationary phases as a population approaches carrying capacity.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'What is carrying capacity?', 'The maximum population size an environment can support sustainably.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'What is predation''s effect on population size?', 'Predator and prey populations fluctuate in cycles, with predator numbers lagging behind prey numbers.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B4', 'Energy, Reproduction and Populations', 'What is a trophic level?', 'A feeding level in a food chain: producers, primary consumers, secondary consumers and so on.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What is the role of DNA polymerase?', 'It joins free DNA nucleotides complementary to a template strand, forming a new strand.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What is splicing?', 'Removing introns from pre-mRNA and joining exons together to form mature mRNA.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What is the role of tRNA?', 'It carries specific amino acids to the ribosome and matches its anticodon to the mRNA codon.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What is the lac operon?', 'A group of genes in E. coli controlling lactose metabolism. A repressor blocks transcription unless lactose is present to bind to it.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What is a dihybrid cross?', 'A cross involving two genes, each with two alleles, e.g. AaBb × AaBb gives a 9:3:3:1 ratio if the genes are unlinked.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What is a sex-linked characteristic?', 'A characteristic controlled by a gene on a sex chromosome (usually X), e.g. colour blindness.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What is the all-or-nothing principle?', 'An action potential is only generated if the stimulus reaches the threshold; all action potentials are the same size.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What is saltatory conduction?', 'Action potentials jump from one node of Ranvier to the next in myelinated neurones, speeding up transmission.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What is the role of the hypothalamus in homeostasis?', 'It monitors blood temperature and water potential and coordinates responses through nerves and hormones.', 9),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What are the effects of insulin on target cells?', 'It increases glucose uptake (more GLUT4 channels), stimulates glycogenesis in liver and muscle, and increases respiration.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What is gluconeogenesis?', 'Making glucose from non-carbohydrate sources such as amino acids and glycerol, in the liver.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What is the role of the collecting duct?', 'Water is reabsorbed in the collecting duct, controlled by ADH, which regulates the concentration of urine.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What happens when the body is too hot?', 'Vasodilation, sweating, hairs lying flat, and reduced metabolic rate.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What happens when the body is too cold?', 'Vasoconstriction, shivering, hairs raised, and increased metabolic rate (via thyroxine and adrenaline).', 14),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B5', 'Genetics, Control and Homeostasis', 'What is a nephron?', 'The functional unit of the kidney where blood is filtered and urine is formed.', 15),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What is the role of restriction enzymes?', 'They cut DNA at specific recognition sequences, often producing sticky ends.', 1),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What is gene sequencing?', 'Determining the order of bases in a gene or genome, e.g. by Sanger sequencing or next-generation methods.', 2),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What is a gene library?', 'A collection of DNA fragments of an organism''s genome, stored in vectors such as bacteria.', 3),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What is the role of microorganisms in food production?', 'Yeast in bread and brewing, bacteria in yoghurt and cheese; they are also used to make mycoprotein.', 4),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What are the advantages of using microorganisms in biotechnology?', 'Fast growth, simple nutritional needs, easy to genetically modify, and they can be grown in large fermenters.', 5),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What conditions are controlled in a fermenter?', 'Temperature, pH, oxygen, nutrients, and aseptic conditions.', 6),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What is downstream processing?', 'Separating and purifying the product from the fermentation mixture.', 7),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What is bioremediation?', 'Using microorganisms to break down pollutants, e.g. oil spills.', 8),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What is the role of decomposers in the nitrogen cycle?', 'They convert nitrogen in dead organisms and waste into ammonium ions (ammonification).', 9),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What is eutrophication?', 'Excess nutrients cause algal blooms, which block light; plants die, decomposers use up oxygen, and aquatic animals die.', 10),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What is a keystone species?', 'A species that has a disproportionately large effect on its ecosystem, e.g. sea otters controlling sea urchins.', 11),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What is the role of hedgerows in biodiversity?', 'They provide habitats and food, and act as corridors for wildlife between habitats.', 12),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What is the effect of monoculture on biodiversity?', 'Growing one crop over large areas reduces habitat and species diversity and increases pest outbreaks.', 13),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What are the ethical issues of GM crops?', 'Concerns over biodiversity, cross-pollination with wild plants, farmers'' dependence on seed companies, and consumer choice.', 14),
  ('Biology', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'B6', 'Biotechnology, Ecosystems and Human Impact', 'What is sustainable fishing?', 'Using quotas, larger mesh sizes and no-fishing zones to keep fish stocks at a level that allows populations to recover.', 15)
),
fresh AS (
  SELECT v.* FROM v
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM flashcards f
    WHERE f.subject = v.subject AND f.exam_board = v.exam_board AND f.level = v.level
      AND f.topic_id = v.topic_id AND f.front = v.front)
),
cur AS (
  SELECT subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, count(*) AS n, max(card_order) AS mx
  FROM flashcards
  WHERE subject = 'Biology' AND exam_board = 'OCR B' AND level = 'alevel'
  GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4
),
ranked AS (
  SELECT fr.*, coalesce(c.n, 0) AS n, coalesce(c.mx, 0) AS mx,
         row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY fr.topic_id ORDER BY fr.ord) AS rn
  FROM fresh fr
  LEFT JOIN cur c USING (subject, exam_board, level, topic_id)
)
INSERT INTO flashcards (subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, card_order, level)
SELECT subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, mx + rn, level
FROM ranked
WHERE n + rn <= 30;

-- Flashcard top-up: Chemistry|AQA|alevel — brings each topic up to 30 cards.
-- Generated by scripts/build-flashcard-topup.js from flashcards/topup/src/chemistry-aqa-alevel.txt
-- Safe to re-run: skips fronts already present and never takes a topic past 30.
WITH v(subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, ord) AS (VALUES
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'What is the relative charge and relative mass of a proton, neutron and electron?', 'Proton +1, mass 1; neutron 0, mass 1; electron −1, mass about 1/1840.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'Why is there a decrease in first ionisation energy from Mg to Al?', 'Al''s outer electron is in a 3p sub-shell, which is higher in energy and slightly more shielded than Mg''s 3s, so it is easier to remove.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'Why do successive ionisation energies always increase?', 'Each electron is removed from an increasingly positive ion, so there is less repulsion and the remaining electrons are held more strongly.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'State Avogadro''s constant and how to calculate moles from mass.', 'N_A = 6.022 × 10^23 mol^-1. Moles = mass (g) ÷ M_r.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'How is concentration calculated from moles and volume?', 'Concentration (mol dm^-3) = moles ÷ volume (dm^3). Divide cm^3 by 1000 to get dm^3.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'Define percentage yield.', '(Actual yield ÷ theoretical yield) × 100%.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'What is ionic bonding?', 'Electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions in a giant lattice.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'What is a dative (coordinate) covalent bond?', 'A covalent bond in which both shared electrons come from the same atom, e.g. in NH4+ or H3O+.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'What is metallic bonding?', 'Electrostatic attraction between a lattice of positive metal ions and a sea of delocalised electrons.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'State the shapes and bond angles for 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 electron pairs (all bonding).', 'Linear 180°; trigonal planar 120°; tetrahedral 109.5°; trigonal bipyramidal 90° and 120°; octahedral 90°.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'Why is the bond angle in H2O (104.5°) smaller than in CH4?', 'H2O has two lone pairs, which repel more strongly than bonding pairs, compressing the H–O–H angle.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'Why is graphite a good conductor but diamond is not?', 'In graphite each C forms 3 bonds, leaving one delocalised electron per atom that can move between layers. In diamond all 4 electrons are used in bonding.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'Define standard enthalpy of formation.', 'The enthalpy change when 1 mole of a compound is formed from its elements in their standard states under standard conditions (100 kPa, 298 K).', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'Define activation energy.', 'The minimum energy that colliding particles need for a reaction to occur.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P1', 'Physical Chemistry I', 'Why does increasing concentration increase rate?', 'There are more particles per unit volume, so collisions happen more often and there are more successful collisions per second.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Physical Chemistry II', 'Define the half-life of a first-order reaction and state a key property.', 'The time taken for the concentration of a reactant to halve; for a first-order reaction it is constant.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Physical Chemistry II', 'How is the order of reaction found from a concentration–time graph?', 'Zero order: straight line. First order: constant half-life. Second order: the half-life increases as concentration falls.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Physical Chemistry II', 'What does a rate–concentration graph look like for zero, first and second order?', 'Zero: horizontal line. First: straight line through the origin. Second: upward curve (parabola).', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Physical Chemistry II', 'How is the rate-determining step linked to the rate equation?', 'Species in the rate equation (with their orders) appear in the rate-determining step; species not in the rate equation react after it.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Physical Chemistry II', 'How is activation energy found graphically from the Arrhenius equation?', 'Plot ln k against 1/T: the gradient is −Ea/R.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Physical Chemistry II', 'Define mole fraction and partial pressure.', 'Mole fraction = moles of gas ÷ total moles of gas. Partial pressure = mole fraction × total pressure.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Physical Chemistry II', 'How does increasing total pressure affect Kp?', 'It has no effect on Kp; only temperature changes Kp. The equilibrium position may shift, but Kp stays the same.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Physical Chemistry II', 'Define standard enthalpy of hydration.', 'The enthalpy change when 1 mole of gaseous ions dissolves in water to form 1 mole of aqueous ions.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Physical Chemistry II', 'How is enthalpy of solution related to lattice and hydration enthalpies?', 'ΔH_solution = −ΔH_lattice formation + ΣΔH_hydration (equivalently lattice dissociation + hydration).', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Physical Chemistry II', 'At what temperature does a reaction become feasible?', 'When ΔG = 0: T = ΔH/ΔS (with ΔS in kJ K^-1 mol^-1 to match ΔH).', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Physical Chemistry II', 'What is the ionic product of water, Kw?', 'Kw = [H+][OH−] = 1.00 × 10^-14 mol^2 dm^-6 at 298 K.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Physical Chemistry II', 'Calculate the pH of 0.050 mol dm^-3 NaOH at 298 K.', '[H+] = 1.0 × 10^-14/0.050 = 2.0 × 10^-13, so pH = 12.7.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Physical Chemistry II', 'Describe the standard hydrogen electrode.', 'Hydrogen gas at 100 kPa bubbled over a platinum electrode in 1.00 mol dm^-3 H+ at 298 K. Its E° is defined as 0.00 V.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'P2', 'Physical Chemistry II', 'Give the half-equations for the lithium-ion cell in outline.', 'Positive electrode: Li+ + CoO2 + e− → Li+[CoO2]−. Negative electrode: Li → Li+ + e−. The reactions are reversed on recharging.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'Why does first ionisation energy generally increase across Period 3?', 'Nuclear charge increases while shielding stays similar, so outer electrons are held more strongly.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'Why does silicon have a very high melting point?', 'It has a giant covalent (macromolecular) structure, so many strong covalent bonds must be broken.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'Why does sulfur (S8) have a higher melting point than phosphorus (P4)?', 'S8 molecules are larger with more electrons, so van der Waals forces between them are stronger.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'Why does first ionisation energy decrease down Group 2?', 'The outer electrons are further from the nucleus and more shielded, so less energy is needed to remove them.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'Give the equation for calcium reacting with water.', 'Ca + 2H2O → Ca(OH)2 + H2.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'Give a use of magnesium hydroxide and calcium hydroxide.', 'Mg(OH)2: an antacid to neutralise excess stomach acid. Ca(OH)2: slaked lime, used to neutralise acidic soils.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'Why is barium sulfate used in barium meals despite barium ions being toxic?', 'BaSO4 is insoluble, so it isn''t absorbed into the blood; it absorbs X-rays, outlining the gut.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'How is titanium extracted using magnesium?', 'TiO2 is converted to TiCl4, which is reduced by Mg at high temperature in an argon atmosphere: TiCl4 + 2Mg → Ti + 2MgCl2.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'How is CaO used to remove SO2 from flue gases?', 'CaO (or CaCO3) reacts with SO2 to form calcium sulfite: CaO + SO2 → CaSO3.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'Why does electronegativity decrease down Group 7?', 'The atomic radius increases and shielding increases, so the attraction of the nucleus for bonding electrons decreases.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'Why do boiling points increase down Group 7?', 'Molecules get larger with more electrons, so van der Waals forces are stronger.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'Give the products of NaBr with concentrated sulfuric acid.', 'HBr (steamy fumes), then Br2 (brown fumes) and SO2, as Br− reduces sulfuric acid from +6 to +4.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'Give the equation for chlorine reacting with water.', 'Cl2 + H2O ⇌ HCl + HClO (a disproportionation; in sunlight 2Cl2 + 2H2O → 4HCl + O2).', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'What is the test for hydroxide ions?', 'Add a few drops of red litmus or universal indicator; it turns blue or purple. Warming with NH4+ also gives ammonia gas.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I1', 'Inorganic Chemistry I', 'How are carbonate ions tested for?', 'Add dilute acid; effervescence gives CO2, which turns limewater cloudy.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'Give the equations for Na2O and MgO with water and the pH values.', 'Na2O + H2O → 2NaOH (pH about 14). MgO + H2O → Mg(OH)2 (pH about 9, as it is only slightly soluble).', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'Give the equation for P4O10 reacting with water.', 'P4O10 + 6H2O → 4H3PO4 (pH about 0).', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'Why is Al2O3 described as amphoteric?', 'It reacts with both acids and bases: with HCl to form AlCl3, and with NaOH to form Na[Al(OH)4].', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'Give the equation for SiO2 reacting with NaOH.', 'SiO2 + 2NaOH → Na2SiO3 + H2O (SiO2 is acidic but insoluble in water).', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'Give four characteristic properties of transition metals.', 'Variable oxidation states, coloured ions, catalytic activity, and the ability to form complex ions.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'What is the shape of [Ag(NH3)2]+ and give its use?', 'Linear (180°). It is Tollens'' reagent, used to test for aldehydes.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'What is the geometry of cisplatin?', 'Square planar, with two Cl− and two NH3 ligands cis to each other.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'How is the colour of a complex ion determined?', 'Ligands split the d orbitals; electrons absorb light of energy ΔE = hν to move between them, and the complementary colour is seen.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'State three factors that change the colour of a transition metal complex.', 'Changing the oxidation state, the ligand, or the coordination number (and so the shape).', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'What is a homogeneous catalyst and give an example?', 'A catalyst in the same phase as the reactants, e.g. Fe2+ in the reaction between I− and S2O8^2−.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'How does Fe2+ catalyse the reaction of I− and S2O8^2−?', 'Fe2+ reduces S2O8^2− to SO4^2−, forming Fe3+; Fe3+ then oxidises I− to I2, regenerating Fe2+. Both steps involve oppositely charged ions.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'What is autocatalysis? Give an example.', 'A reaction catalysed by one of its products, e.g. Mn2+ catalysing the reaction of MnO4− with C2O4^2−.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'Give the colour and formula of the precipitate when NaOH is added to Fe2+(aq) and Fe3+(aq).', 'Fe(OH)2(H2O)4: green precipitate (darkens on standing). Fe(OH)3(H2O)3: brown/orange precipitate.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'What happens when Na2CO3 is added to [Al(H2O)6]3+?', 'A white precipitate of Al(OH)3(H2O)3 forms with effervescence (CO2), because the 3+ ion is acidic enough to release CO2.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'I2', 'Inorganic Chemistry II', 'How is manganate(VII) titration used to find the concentration of Fe2+?', 'Titrate acidified Fe2+ with KMnO4 (self-indicating: the endpoint is the first permanent pink). MnO4− + 8H+ + 5Fe2+ → Mn2+ + 4H2O + 5Fe3+.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What is a functional group?', 'An atom or group of atoms that gives a compound its characteristic chemical properties.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What is chain isomerism?', 'Isomers with the same molecular formula but different carbon skeletons (branching).', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What is position isomerism?', 'Isomers with the same carbon skeleton and functional group, but with the functional group in a different position.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What is fractional distillation of crude oil?', 'Separating crude oil into fractions by boiling point in a column that is hotter at the bottom and cooler at the top.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'Compare thermal and catalytic cracking.', 'Thermal: high temperature and pressure, producing alkenes. Catalytic: zeolite catalyst at a slight pressure, producing branched alkanes, cycloalkanes and aromatics for fuels.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What pollutants are formed from burning alkanes, and how are they removed?', 'CO, NOx and unburnt hydrocarbons, removed by a catalytic converter (Pt/Rh). SO2 is removed by flue gas desulfurisation.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'Why do CFCs damage the ozone layer?', 'UV light breaks C–Cl bonds, forming Cl radicals, which catalyse the breakdown of O3: Cl• + O3 → ClO• + O2; ClO• + O3 → 2O2 + Cl•.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'Give the reagent and conditions to form a nitrile from a halogenoalkane.', 'KCN in ethanol and water, heated under reflux (nucleophilic substitution, adding a carbon).', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'Give the reagent and conditions to form an amine from a halogenoalkane.', 'Excess ethanolic ammonia, heated under pressure (nucleophilic substitution).', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'Describe the mechanism of electrophilic addition of Br2 to ethene.', 'The π bond attacks Br (the Br–Br bond is polarised); a carbocation and Br− form; Br− attacks the carbocation to give 1,2-dibromoethane.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'How is ethanol produced industrially by hydration of ethene?', 'Ethene and steam over a phosphoric acid catalyst at about 300 °C and 60–70 atm.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What are the advantages and disadvantages of producing ethanol by fermentation?', 'Advantages: renewable, low energy, can be called carbon neutral. Disadvantages: slow, batch process, impure product, uses land.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'How is an alcohol dehydrated to an alkene?', 'Heat with concentrated phosphoric or sulfuric acid catalyst (elimination).', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What are the fingerprint region and its use in IR?', 'Below 1500 cm^-1; its complex pattern is unique to each molecule, so comparing with a database identifies it.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O1', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What is high-resolution mass spectrometry used for?', 'Measuring M_r to four or more decimal places to tell apart molecules with the same nominal mass but different molecular formulae.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'What is optical isomerism and what feature causes it?', 'Stereoisomers that are non-superimposable mirror images, caused by a chiral carbon with four different groups.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'What is a racemic mixture and why is it optically inactive?', 'A 50:50 mixture of two enantiomers. Their opposite rotations of plane-polarised light cancel.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'Why does nucleophilic addition of HCN to an aldehyde give a racemic mixture?', 'The C=O is planar, so CN− can attack from either side with equal probability.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'How is an acyl chloride reacted with ammonia and what is formed?', 'Nucleophilic addition–elimination at room temperature gives an amide and HCl (which reacts with more NH3 to form NH4Cl).', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'Why are acid anhydrides preferred to acyl chlorides in the manufacture of aspirin?', 'They are cheaper, less corrosive, react less violently with water, and don''t produce corrosive HCl.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'Give the conditions for acid and base hydrolysis of esters.', 'Acid: heat under reflux with dilute acid (reversible). Base: heat under reflux with NaOH, giving a carboxylate salt and alcohol (goes to completion).', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'How is biodiesel made?', 'Transesterification of vegetable oils (triglycerides) with methanol, using a KOH catalyst.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'Describe the mechanism for nitration of benzene.', 'NO2+ (from conc. HNO3 and H2SO4 at 50 °C) is attacked by the benzene π system, giving an intermediate that loses H+ to restore aromaticity.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'How is an amine formed by reducing a nitrile?', 'With LiAlH4 in dry ether, or with H2 and a Ni catalyst: RCN + 4[H] → RCH2NH2.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'Why are quaternary ammonium salts useful?', 'As cationic surfactants in fabric softeners and hair conditioners.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'What is the structure of a condensation polyamide such as nylon-6,6?', 'Formed from a diamine and a dicarboxylic acid (or diacyl chloride), with amide links (–CONH–) and water (or HCl) lost.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'Why is Kevlar so strong?', 'Its rigid, linear chains of benzene rings are held together by many hydrogen bonds between chains.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'Describe the primary, secondary and tertiary structure of proteins.', 'Primary: sequence of amino acids. Secondary: α-helix or β-pleated sheet held by H bonds. Tertiary: 3D folding held by H bonds, ionic bonds and disulfide bridges.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'How does 13C NMR differ from 1H NMR?', '13C NMR gives one peak per carbon environment, with no splitting, over a range of about 0–220 ppm.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'AQA', 'alevel', 'O2', 'Organic Chemistry II', 'Describe column chromatography.', 'A column is packed with a stationary phase (silica or alumina); a solvent (mobile phase) carries the mixture through, and components separate by relative affinity.', 15)
),
fresh AS (
  SELECT v.* FROM v
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM flashcards f
    WHERE f.subject = v.subject AND f.exam_board = v.exam_board AND f.level = v.level
      AND f.topic_id = v.topic_id AND f.front = v.front)
),
cur AS (
  SELECT subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, count(*) AS n, max(card_order) AS mx
  FROM flashcards
  WHERE subject = 'Chemistry' AND exam_board = 'AQA' AND level = 'alevel'
  GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4
),
ranked AS (
  SELECT fr.*, coalesce(c.n, 0) AS n, coalesce(c.mx, 0) AS mx,
         row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY fr.topic_id ORDER BY fr.ord) AS rn
  FROM fresh fr
  LEFT JOIN cur c USING (subject, exam_board, level, topic_id)
)
INSERT INTO flashcards (subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, card_order, level)
SELECT subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, mx + rn, level
FROM ranked
WHERE n + rn <= 30;

-- Flashcard top-up: Chemistry|Edexcel|alevel — brings each topic up to 30 cards.
-- Generated by scripts/build-flashcard-topup.js from flashcards/topup/src/chemistry-edexcel-alevel.txt
-- Safe to re-run: skips fronts already present and never takes a topic past 30.
WITH v(subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, ord) AS (VALUES
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'Define relative atomic mass.', 'The weighted mean mass of an atom of an element relative to 1/12 of the mass of a carbon-12 atom.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'What is an orbital?', 'A region around the nucleus that can hold up to two electrons with opposite spins.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'Give the electron configurations of Cr and Cu.', 'Cr: [Ar] 3d5 4s1; Cu: [Ar] 3d10 4s1 (a half-filled or filled 3d sub-shell is more stable).', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'Why does ionisation energy decrease from N to O?', 'In O, two electrons share a 2p orbital; the extra repulsion makes one easier to remove.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'What is the difference between ionic and covalent bonding?', 'Ionic: electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions. Covalent: a shared pair of electrons attracted to both nuclei.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'How does polarisation of an anion produce covalent character in ionic bonds?', 'A small, highly charged cation distorts the electron cloud of a large anion, pulling electron density between the ions.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'What is a σ bond and a π bond?', 'σ: head-on overlap of orbitals along the internuclear axis. π: sideways overlap of p orbitals above and below the axis.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'What are London forces?', 'Temporary induced dipole–dipole attractions caused by fluctuating electron distributions. They are stronger for molecules with more electrons.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'What conditions are needed for hydrogen bonding?', 'H bonded to N, O or F, and a lone pair on an N, O or F atom in another molecule.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'How does the shape of an XeF4 molecule arise?', '4 bonding pairs and 2 lone pairs around Xe give a square planar shape (90°).', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'Why do metals conduct electricity and heat?', 'Delocalised electrons can move through the structure carrying charge and energy.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'Define standard enthalpy of neutralisation.', 'The enthalpy change when 1 mole of water is formed from the reaction of an acid and an alkali under standard conditions.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'How is the enthalpy change of a reaction found from bond enthalpies?', 'ΔH = Σ(bonds broken) − Σ(bonds formed).', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'What is meant by exothermic and endothermic in terms of ΔH?', 'Exothermic: ΔH negative (heat released to the surroundings). Endothermic: ΔH positive (heat absorbed).', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T1', 'Atomic Structure, Bonding and Energetics', 'How do you calculate moles of gas at room temperature from volume?', 'Moles = volume (dm^3) ÷ 24.0 dm^3 mol^-1 at RTP.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'What is the collision theory explanation for the effect of temperature on rate?', 'Particles have more kinetic energy, so a much greater proportion of collisions have energy at least Ea, and collisions are more frequent.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'What is a heterogeneous catalyst?', 'A catalyst in a different phase from the reactants, e.g. iron in the Haber process; the reaction takes place at active sites on its surface.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'How do catalysts increase rate?', 'They provide an alternative reaction pathway with a lower activation energy.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'Give the units of the rate constant for a second-order reaction.', 'dm^3 mol^-1 s^-1.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'What is the initial-rates method?', 'Measuring the rate at the start of several experiments with different starting concentrations to find the order with respect to each reactant.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'How does a clock reaction measure rate?', 'The time for a set amount of product to form (e.g. a sudden colour change) is measured; rate ∝ 1/t.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'Why does increasing pressure shift the equilibrium N2 + 3H2 ⇌ 2NH3 to the right?', 'The right-hand side has fewer moles of gas, so the equilibrium shifts to reduce the pressure.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'What is the effect of adding a catalyst on equilibrium?', 'Equilibrium is reached faster, but its position and K are unchanged.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'Define oxidation and reduction in terms of electrons.', 'Oxidation is loss of electrons; reduction is gain of electrons (OIL RIG).', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'What is a disproportionation reaction?', 'A reaction in which the same element is both oxidised and reduced.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'How is the oxidation state of an element in a compound determined?', 'Oxidation states add up to the overall charge. Fixed values: O is −2 (except in peroxides), H is +1 (except in metal hydrides), F is −1.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'What is a salt bridge and why is it needed in a cell?', 'A strip of filter paper soaked in saturated KNO3 that completes the circuit by allowing ions to move, without the solutions mixing.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'How are cell diagrams written?', 'Pt | Fe2+, Fe3+ || Ag+ | Ag: the oxidation half-cell on the left, the reduction on the right, with a double line for the salt bridge.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'What is the relationship between E°cell and ΔG?', 'ΔG = −nFE°cell; a positive E°cell gives a negative ΔG (feasible).', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T2', 'Kinetics, Equilibrium and Redox', 'What is a fuel cell?', 'A cell that converts the chemical energy of a fuel (e.g. hydrogen) and oxygen directly into electrical energy, producing water.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'Give the equation for sodium reacting with oxygen.', '4Na + O2 → 2Na2O.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'Why does the reactivity of Group 1 metals increase down the group?', 'The outer electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so it is lost more easily.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'Give the equation for the thermal decomposition of lithium nitrate.', '4LiNO3 → 2Li2O + 4NO2 + O2 (Li behaves like Group 2, unlike other Group 1 nitrates).', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'Give the equation for thermal decomposition of sodium nitrate.', '2NaNO3 → 2NaNO2 + O2.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'Why are Group 2 carbonates more thermally stable down the group?', 'Larger cations with the same charge have lower charge density, so they polarise the carbonate ion less.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'What are the flame test colours for Group 2?', 'Ca: brick red; Sr: crimson; Ba: apple green; Mg: no colour.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'What is the trend in reactivity of the halogens, and how is it shown?', 'Reactivity decreases down the group. A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive halide from solution.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'Give the colours of Cl2, Br2 and I2 in water and in cyclohexane.', 'Cl2: pale green in both. Br2: orange in water, orange/red in cyclohexane. I2: brown in water, purple in cyclohexane.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'Give the equation for iodine reacting with thiosulfate in a titration.', 'I2 + 2S2O3^2− → 2I− + S4O6^2−. Starch is added near the endpoint; blue-black turns colourless.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'Why is Cr2O7^2− orange and CrO4^2− yellow?', 'They are different chromium(VI) species in equilibrium. Adding acid gives orange dichromate; adding alkali gives yellow chromate.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'Give the colours of V in its four oxidation states.', 'VO2+ (+5): yellow; VO2+ (+4): blue; V3+ (+3): green; V2+ (+2): violet.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'How is vanadium reduced from +5 to +2?', 'Zinc and acid: the colour changes yellow → blue → green → violet.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'Describe the reaction of [Cr(H2O)6]3+ with excess NaOH.', 'A grey-green precipitate of Cr(OH)3(H2O)3 forms, which dissolves in excess to give dark green [Cr(OH)6]3−.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'What is the shape of a [CuCl4]2− ion?', 'Tetrahedral (Cl− ligands are large, so only four fit).', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T3', 'Inorganic Chemistry', 'What is the test for sulfate ions?', 'Add acidified BaCl2 solution; a white precipitate of BaSO4 forms.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What is a structural formula?', 'A formula showing the arrangement of atoms in a molecule without all the bonds, e.g. CH3CH2OH.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What is a skeletal formula?', 'A formula showing the carbon skeleton as lines, with functional groups shown and H atoms on carbons omitted.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What is geometric (E/Z) isomerism?', 'Stereoisomerism caused by restricted rotation about a C=C bond, where each carbon has two different groups.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'How are E and Z assigned using CIP rules?', 'Find the higher-priority group (higher atomic number) on each carbon of the C=C. Z: both on the same side. E: on opposite sides.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'Why are alkanes unreactive?', 'C–C and C–H bonds are strong and non-polar.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What are the three stages of free-radical substitution?', 'Initiation (UV splits Cl2 into radicals), propagation (radicals react with molecules and are regenerated), termination (two radicals combine).', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What are the problems with free-radical substitution for synthesis?', 'It gives a mixture of products, including further substitution and isomers.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What is formed when an alkene reacts with hydrogen?', 'An alkane, with a nickel catalyst at 150 °C (hydrogenation).', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What is formed when an alkene is oxidised by acidified KMnO4?', 'A diol; the purple solution turns colourless.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'Describe addition polymerisation.', 'Many alkene monomers join by breaking their C=C π bonds to form a long saturated polymer chain.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What are the environmental problems with polymers and the solutions?', 'They are non-biodegradable and fill landfill. Solutions: recycling, incinerating for energy, and biodegradable polymers.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What is the mechanism for OH− with a primary halogenoalkane?', 'SN2: OH− attacks the δ+ carbon as the halide leaves in one step.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'How are halogenoalkanes compared using silver nitrate in ethanol?', 'The precipitate forms fastest for iodo, then bromo, then chloro, because the C–I bond is weakest.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'What is the product of heating an alcohol with PCl5?', 'A chloroalkane, POCl3 and HCl (steamy fumes; also a test for –OH).', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T4', 'Organic Chemistry I', 'How is a secondary alcohol oxidised, and what is observed?', 'Acidified K2Cr2O7, heated under reflux, gives a ketone; the colour turns from orange to green.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'What is the Born–Haber cycle used for?', 'Calculating a lattice energy from other measurable enthalpy changes using Hess''s law.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'Define first electron affinity.', 'The enthalpy change when 1 mole of gaseous atoms each gains one electron to form 1 mole of gaseous 1− ions.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'Why is the second electron affinity of oxygen endothermic?', 'The electron is added to a negative ion, so energy is needed to overcome the repulsion.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'Define enthalpy change of atomisation.', 'The enthalpy change when 1 mole of gaseous atoms is formed from an element in its standard state.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'What does entropy measure?', 'The number of ways particles and their energy can be arranged (disorder).', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'Give the equation for the total entropy change.', 'ΔS_total = ΔS_system + ΔS_surroundings, where ΔS_surroundings = −ΔH/T.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'State the relationship between ΔG and K.', 'ΔG = −RT ln K.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'Define a Brønsted–Lowry acid and base.', 'Acid: proton donor. Base: proton acceptor.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'What is a conjugate acid–base pair?', 'Two species differing by one proton, e.g. CH3COOH and CH3COO−.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'Calculate the pH of 0.020 mol dm^-3 HCl.', 'pH = −log(0.020) = 1.70.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'Why is pure water neutral at all temperatures even though pH changes?', '[H+] always equals [OH−], even though Kw (and so pH) changes with temperature.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'How is Ka found from a half-neutralisation point?', 'At half-neutralisation [HA] = [A−], so pH = pKa.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'Give two uses of buffers.', 'Maintaining blood pH (carbonic acid/hydrogencarbonate) and shampoos, food and biological experiments.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'Why is phenolphthalein unsuitable for a weak base–strong acid titration?', 'Its colour change range (pH 8.3–10) is not within the steep vertical section, which is in the acidic region.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T5', 'Thermodynamics, Acids & Bases', 'How does temperature affect the pH of pure water?', 'Ionisation is endothermic, so higher temperatures increase Kw and lower the pH, although the water stays neutral.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'What is the delocalised model of benzene?', 'A planar ring of 6 carbons, each sp2, with a ring of 6 delocalised π electrons above and below the plane; all C–C bonds are equal length.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'Give evidence against the Kekulé structure.', 'All C–C bonds are the same length, the enthalpy of hydrogenation is less exothermic than predicted, and benzene resists addition reactions.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'Describe Friedel–Crafts alkylation.', 'A halogenoalkane with an AlCl3 catalyst forms a carbocation electrophile, which substitutes onto the benzene ring.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'Give the reaction of phenol with bromine water.', 'White precipitate of 2,4,6-tribromophenol at room temperature, without a catalyst; bromine water is decolourised.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'How are aldehydes and ketones reduced?', 'With LiAlH4 in dry ether (or NaBH4): aldehydes give primary alcohols and ketones give secondary alcohols.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'Give the test for methyl ketones.', 'The iodoform test: warm with iodine in alkali; a pale yellow precipitate of CHI3 forms.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'How do carboxylic acids react with carbonates?', 'They form a salt, water and CO2 (effervescence).', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'How are acyl chlorides hydrolysed?', 'They react vigorously with cold water to form a carboxylic acid and steamy fumes of HCl.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'Give the mechanism type for acyl chloride reactions.', 'Nucleophilic addition–elimination.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'How is an amide formed from an acyl chloride?', 'React with ammonia (primary amide) or an amine (N-substituted amide).', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'What is the difference between primary, secondary and tertiary amines?', 'One, two or three hydrogen atoms of ammonia replaced by alkyl or aryl groups.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'How are amino acids separated and identified?', 'By TLC or paper chromatography, using ninhydrin to show spots and comparing Rf values with known samples.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'How do you plan a multi-step synthesis?', 'Identify the functional groups in the starting material and product, then use known reactions (e.g. substitution, oxidation, reduction) to link them, adding a carbon with KCN if needed.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'What is the purpose of recrystallisation?', 'To purify a solid: dissolve it in the minimum hot solvent, filter hot, cool to crystallise, then filter and dry.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'Edexcel', 'alevel', 'T6', 'Advanced Organic & Analysis', 'How is melting point used to check purity?', 'A pure substance melts sharply at its known melting point; impurities lower it and broaden the range.', 15)
),
fresh AS (
  SELECT v.* FROM v
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM flashcards f
    WHERE f.subject = v.subject AND f.exam_board = v.exam_board AND f.level = v.level
      AND f.topic_id = v.topic_id AND f.front = v.front)
),
cur AS (
  SELECT subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, count(*) AS n, max(card_order) AS mx
  FROM flashcards
  WHERE subject = 'Chemistry' AND exam_board = 'Edexcel' AND level = 'alevel'
  GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4
),
ranked AS (
  SELECT fr.*, coalesce(c.n, 0) AS n, coalesce(c.mx, 0) AS mx,
         row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY fr.topic_id ORDER BY fr.ord) AS rn
  FROM fresh fr
  LEFT JOIN cur c USING (subject, exam_board, level, topic_id)
)
INSERT INTO flashcards (subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, card_order, level)
SELECT subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, mx + rn, level
FROM ranked
WHERE n + rn <= 30;

-- Flashcard top-up: Chemistry|OCR A|alevel — brings each topic up to 30 cards.
-- Generated by scripts/build-flashcard-topup.js from flashcards/topup/src/chemistry-ocr-a-alevel.txt
-- Safe to re-run: skips fronts already present and never takes a topic past 30.
WITH v(subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, ord) AS (VALUES
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'How should a burette be read?', 'At eye level, from the bottom of the meniscus, to the nearest 0.05 cm^3.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is the uncertainty in a titre from a burette?', 'Each reading is ±0.05 cm^3; a titre uses two readings, so ±0.10 cm^3.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'Why is a white tile used in a titration?', 'It makes the colour change at the end point easier to see.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'Why is the conical flask swirled during a titration?', 'To mix the reactants thoroughly, so the end point isn''t overshot locally.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'How is a separating funnel used to purify an organic liquid?', 'Shake the organic layer with an aqueous solution (e.g. sodium carbonate to remove acid), release pressure, allow the layers to separate, then run off the lower layer.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is the role of anhydrous MgSO4 or CaCl2 in organic preparations?', 'A drying agent that removes water from an organic liquid. It is added until it no longer clumps, then filtered off.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'How is a Buchner funnel used?', 'Vacuum (suction) filtration to separate a solid from a liquid quickly, leaving a dry solid on the filter paper.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'How is melting point used to test purity?', 'Pure substances melt sharply at a known temperature; impurities lower the melting point and widen the range.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'Why are anti-bumping granules used when heating?', 'They promote smooth boiling, preventing large bubbles that could cause violent bumping.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is simple distillation used for?', 'Separating a liquid from a solution or from liquids with very different boiling points, collecting the product at its boiling point.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'How is the enthalpy change of combustion measured using a spirit burner?', 'Weigh the burner, heat a known mass of water in a copper can, record the temperature rise and reweigh. q = mcΔT; divide by moles burned.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'How is the rate of a gas-producing reaction measured?', 'Collect the gas in a gas syringe (or measure mass lost on a balance) at regular time intervals.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'What is the purpose of a trial (rough) titration?', 'To find the approximate end point, so accurate titrations can add drops carefully near it.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'How are anomalies identified in titration data?', 'Titres that aren''t concordant (within 0.10 cm^3 of the others) are excluded from the mean.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M1', 'Development of Practical Skills', 'How can heat loss in calorimetry be accounted for?', 'Plot temperature against time and extrapolate back to the time of mixing to estimate the maximum temperature change.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'What is the relative formula mass?', 'The sum of the relative atomic masses of all the atoms in the formula of an ionic compound.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'How is water of crystallisation determined experimentally?', 'Heat a weighed hydrated salt to constant mass. The mass lost is water; find the mole ratio of anhydrous salt to water.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'What is the molar gas volume at RTP?', '24.0 dm^3 mol^-1 (at about 20 °C and 101 kPa).', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'How is the ideal gas equation used to find M_r?', 'n = pV/RT, then M_r = mass/n. Use Pa, m^3 and K.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'What is a strong acid?', 'An acid that dissociates completely in water, e.g. HCl, HNO3, H2SO4.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'What is a weak acid?', 'An acid that only partially dissociates in water, e.g. CH3COOH.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'What are the products of an acid with a metal oxide and with ammonia?', 'Metal oxide: salt and water. Ammonia: an ammonium salt only.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'What is a sub-shell and how many electrons can s, p and d sub-shells hold?', 'A group of orbitals of the same type within a shell: s holds 2, p holds 6, d holds 10.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'Why is the 4s sub-shell filled before 3d?', 'The 4s sub-shell is lower in energy than 3d in the atoms before scandium, so it fills first (and empties first when ions form).', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'What is an ionic lattice and give its properties?', 'A giant 3D arrangement of oppositely charged ions. High melting points, soluble in polar solvents, and conduct only when molten or dissolved.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'What is a simple molecular substance and give its properties?', 'Small molecules held by weak intermolecular forces. Low melting and boiling points, don''t conduct, often soluble in non-polar solvents.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'What is a permanent dipole–dipole interaction?', 'An attraction between permanent dipoles in neighbouring polar molecules (δ+ of one to δ− of another).', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'Why is CO2 non-polar despite polar bonds?', 'It is linear and symmetrical, so the bond dipoles cancel.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'Give the shape of SF6 and its bond angle.', 'Octahedral, 90°.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M2', 'Foundations in Chemistry', 'What is a redox reaction in terms of oxidation number?', 'A reaction in which one species'' oxidation number increases (oxidation) and another''s decreases (reduction).', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'What is periodicity?', 'A repeating trend in physical or chemical properties across periods of the periodic table.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'Why do melting points increase from Na to Al?', 'Metallic bonding gets stronger: more delocalised electrons per atom and smaller, more highly charged ions.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'Why does the first ionisation energy drop from Be to B?', 'B''s outer electron is in a 2p sub-shell, higher in energy than 2s, so it is easier to remove.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'Why does reactivity of Group 2 metals increase down the group?', 'The ionisation energies decrease (more shielding, larger atoms), so the metals lose electrons more easily.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'Give the equation for calcium reacting with oxygen.', '2Ca + O2 → 2CaO.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'Why is calcium hydroxide used in agriculture?', 'To neutralise acidic soils.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'What is the test for halide ions?', 'Add dilute HNO3 then AgNO3(aq). Cl−: white precipitate; Br−: cream; I−: yellow. Then check solubility in dilute or concentrated NH3.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'What is a disproportionation reaction? Give a Group 7 example.', 'The same element is simultaneously oxidised and reduced, e.g. Cl2 + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H2O.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'What are the risks and benefits of chlorinating water?', 'Benefit: kills bacteria, preventing disease. Risks: chlorine is toxic, and it can form chlorinated hydrocarbons that may cause cancer.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'Give tests for carbonate, sulfate and halide ions and the order to do them.', 'Carbonate (dilute HNO3, test gas with limewater), then sulfate (Ba(NO3)2), then halide (AgNO3). This order avoids false positives.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'Define average bond enthalpy.', 'The mean energy needed to break 1 mole of a given type of bond in gaseous molecules, averaged over many compounds.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'Why is bond breaking endothermic?', 'Energy is needed to overcome the attraction between the bonding electrons and the nuclei.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'How is the rate of a reaction found from a concentration–time graph?', 'From the gradient of a tangent at that time.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'What is dynamic equilibrium?', 'The forward and reverse reaction rates are equal, so the concentrations of reactants and products stay constant, in a closed system.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M3', 'Periodic Table and Energy', 'What does Kc tell you about the position of equilibrium?', 'Kc > 1: equilibrium lies to the right (products favoured). Kc < 1: equilibrium lies to the left.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'What is the general formula of the alkanes, alkenes and alcohols?', 'Alkanes CnH2n+2; alkenes CnH2n; alcohols CnH2n+1OH.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'What is a saturated and an unsaturated hydrocarbon?', 'Saturated: only C–C single bonds. Unsaturated: at least one C=C (or C≡C) multiple bond.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'What is homolytic fission?', 'A covalent bond breaks so each atom takes one electron, forming two radicals.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'What is heterolytic fission?', 'A covalent bond breaks so one atom takes both electrons, forming a cation and an anion.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'What is a nucleophile?', 'An electron-pair donor, e.g. OH−, CN−, NH3.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'What is an electrophile?', 'An electron-pair acceptor, e.g. H+, NO2+, Br+ (δ+ Br).', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'Why do branched alkanes have lower boiling points than straight-chain isomers?', 'Branching reduces the surface contact between molecules, so London forces are weaker.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'What is the C=C made of and why is rotation restricted?', 'A σ bond and a π bond. The π bond, from sideways p-orbital overlap, prevents rotation.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'Describe the mechanism for electrophilic addition of HBr to propene.', 'The π electrons attack H of δ+H–Brδ−; the more stable secondary carbocation forms; Br− attacks to give mainly 2-bromopropane.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'Why is a tertiary carbocation more stable than a primary one?', 'Alkyl groups donate electron density (inductive effect), spreading out the positive charge.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'Give the conditions for oxidising a primary alcohol to an aldehyde.', 'Acidified K2Cr2O7, heated gently, distilling off the aldehyde as it forms.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'Why are tertiary alcohols not oxidised by acidified dichromate?', 'There is no hydrogen on the carbon bonded to the –OH.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'Give the reaction for making a bromoalkane from an alcohol.', 'Heat the alcohol with NaBr and concentrated H2SO4 (which make HBr): ROH + HBr → RBr + H2O.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'What does the molecular ion peak show in a mass spectrum?', 'The M_r of the compound (the peak with the highest m/z, apart from M+1).', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M4', 'Core Organic Chemistry', 'Why is the reaction of halogenoalkanes with hot aqueous NaOH called hydrolysis?', 'The C–X bond is broken by reaction with OH− (from water) to form an alcohol.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'Define the half-life of a first-order reaction.', 'The time taken for the concentration of a reactant to fall to half its value. It is constant for first order.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'How is k found from the half-life of a first-order reaction?', 'k = ln 2 / t½.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'What is the rate-determining step?', 'The slowest step in a multi-step mechanism, which controls the overall rate.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'How is Ea found from an Arrhenius plot?', 'Plot ln k against 1/T: gradient = −Ea/R.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'Define standard enthalpy of hydration.', 'The enthalpy change when 1 mole of gaseous ions dissolves in water to form aqueous ions.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'How does ionic size and charge affect lattice enthalpy?', 'Smaller ions and higher charges give stronger attraction, so the lattice enthalpy is more exothermic.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'When is a reaction with ΔH < 0 and ΔS < 0 feasible?', 'At low temperatures, where |ΔH| > |TΔS|, so ΔG < 0.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'Calculate the pH of a buffer with 0.10 mol dm^-3 CH3COOH and 0.20 mol dm^-3 CH3COONa (Ka = 1.8 × 10^-5).', '[H+] = Ka × [HA]/[A−] = 1.8 × 10^-5 × 0.5 = 9.0 × 10^-6, so pH = 5.05.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'How does the carbonic acid–hydrogencarbonate buffer control blood pH?', 'H2CO3 ⇌ H+ + HCO3−. Added acid is removed by HCO3−; added alkali is removed by H2CO3. The pH stays about 7.4.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'What is the standard hydrogen electrode?', 'H2 gas at 100 kPa bubbled over platinum in 1 mol dm^-3 H+ at 298 K; E° = 0.00 V.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'How do you predict the feasibility of a redox reaction from E° values?', 'The more positive half-cell is reduced and the more negative one is oxidised. If E°cell is positive, the reaction is feasible.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'What is a stereoisomer of a complex ion? Give an example.', 'Cis–trans (e.g. cis- and trans-platin, square planar) or optical isomers (e.g. [Ni(en)3]2+, octahedral with bidentate ligands).', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'Give the colour of [Co(H2O)6]2+ and [CoCl4]2−.', 'Pink and blue respectively. Adding concentrated HCl changes pink to blue.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'What happens when NaOH is added to Mn2+(aq) and Cr3+(aq)?', 'Mn2+: light brown precipitate, darkening in air. Cr3+: grey-green precipitate, which dissolves in excess to give dark green [Cr(OH)6]3−.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M5', 'Physical Chemistry & Transition Elements', 'How is iodine–thiosulfate titration used to find the concentration of an oxidising agent?', 'The oxidising agent releases I2 from excess I−; titrate the I2 with S2O3^2−, adding starch near the end point (blue-black to colourless).', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'What is the evidence that benzene has delocalised electrons?', 'All C–C bonds are the same length (0.139 nm), hydrogenation is less exothermic than expected, and benzene resists addition reactions.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'Describe the mechanism for bromination of benzene.', 'Br2 with FeBr3 forms Br+ (and FeBr4−); benzene''s π system attacks Br+; the intermediate loses H+, which regenerates FeBr3 and forms HBr.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'Why is phenol more reactive than benzene?', 'A lone pair on the O is partially delocalised into the ring, increasing the electron density so it can polarise electrophiles.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'What are directing effects on a benzene ring?', 'Electron-donating groups (–OH, –NH2) direct to the 2- and 4-positions; electron-withdrawing groups (–NO2) direct to the 3-position.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'How is NaBH4 used to reduce carbonyls?', 'NaBH4 in water or methanol provides the H− nucleophile. Aldehydes give primary alcohols; ketones give secondary alcohols.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'How is Tollens'' reagent used?', 'Warm with the sample: aldehydes give a silver mirror, ketones give no change.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'Give the products of reacting carboxylic acids with alcohols.', 'An ester and water, catalysed by concentrated H2SO4 and heated under reflux.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'How are amides formed?', 'From acyl chlorides with ammonia (primary amides) or amines (secondary amides).', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'What is a chiral carbon?', 'A carbon atom attached to four different groups, giving non-superimposable mirror images.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'How is a polyester formed?', 'By condensation of a diol and a dicarboxylic acid (or a hydroxycarboxylic acid), with loss of water.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'Why can polyesters and polyamides be broken down by hydrolysis?', 'The ester and amide links are polar and can be attacked by acid or base.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'How are carbon chains lengthened in synthesis?', 'By nucleophilic substitution of a halogenoalkane with CN−, or addition of HCN to a carbonyl, forming nitriles.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'What is formed when a nitrile is hydrolysed?', 'A carboxylic acid (with dilute acid under reflux).', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'What does chemical shift tell you in NMR?', 'The chemical environment of the nucleus, measured in ppm relative to TMS.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR A', 'alevel', 'M6', 'Organic Chemistry & Analysis', 'What is the purpose of thin-layer chromatography?', 'To separate and identify components of a mixture by comparing Rf values with known substances.', 15)
),
fresh AS (
  SELECT v.* FROM v
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM flashcards f
    WHERE f.subject = v.subject AND f.exam_board = v.exam_board AND f.level = v.level
      AND f.topic_id = v.topic_id AND f.front = v.front)
),
cur AS (
  SELECT subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, count(*) AS n, max(card_order) AS mx
  FROM flashcards
  WHERE subject = 'Chemistry' AND exam_board = 'OCR A' AND level = 'alevel'
  GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4
),
ranked AS (
  SELECT fr.*, coalesce(c.n, 0) AS n, coalesce(c.mx, 0) AS mx,
         row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY fr.topic_id ORDER BY fr.ord) AS rn
  FROM fresh fr
  LEFT JOIN cur c USING (subject, exam_board, level, topic_id)
)
INSERT INTO flashcards (subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, card_order, level)
SELECT subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, mx + rn, level
FROM ranked
WHERE n + rn <= 30;

-- Flashcard top-up: Chemistry|OCR B|alevel — brings each topic up to 30 cards.
-- Generated by scripts/build-flashcard-topup.js from flashcards/topup/src/chemistry-ocr-b-alevel.txt
-- Safe to re-run: skips fronts already present and never takes a topic past 30.
WITH v(subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, ord) AS (VALUES
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'Define standard enthalpy of formation.', 'The enthalpy change when 1 mole of a compound is formed from its elements in their standard states under standard conditions.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'Why are standard enthalpies of formation of elements zero?', 'By definition, no change occurs in forming an element from itself in its standard state.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'Give the calorimetry equation for energy transferred.', 'q = mcΔT, where m is the mass of water heated, c is 4.18 J g^-1 K^-1, and ΔT is the temperature change.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'What is bond enthalpy?', 'The energy needed to break 1 mole of a bond in gaseous molecules. Bond breaking is endothermic; bond making is exothermic.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'Why is octane rating important for petrol?', 'A higher octane number means less auto-ignition (knocking); branched alkanes, cycloalkanes and arenes have higher octane numbers.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'What is reforming?', 'Converting straight-chain alkanes into branched alkanes, cycloalkanes and arenes (using a Pt catalyst) to improve octane number.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'What is isomerisation in fuel production?', 'Heating straight-chain alkanes with a catalyst to produce branched isomers.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'What is cracking and why is it done?', 'Breaking long-chain alkanes into shorter alkanes and alkenes; it matches supply to demand and produces alkenes for polymers.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'Name the pollutants from car engines and their harmful effects.', 'CO (toxic), NOx (acid rain, photochemical smog), unburnt hydrocarbons (smog), particulates (respiratory problems), CO2 (climate change).', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'How does a catalytic converter work?', 'Pt/Rh on a honeycomb: CO and hydrocarbons are oxidised to CO2 and H2O, and NOx is reduced to N2.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'What is catalyst poisoning?', 'Impurities (e.g. lead) bond strongly to the active sites of a heterogeneous catalyst and block them.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'How is a structural isomer different from a stereoisomer?', 'Structural isomers have different atom connectivity; stereoisomers have the same connectivity but a different spatial arrangement.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'What are the shapes around carbon in alkanes and alkenes?', 'Tetrahedral (109.5°) around sp3 carbon; trigonal planar (120°) around the C=C carbons.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'What is the difference between a σ and a π bond?', 'σ: direct overlap on the internuclear axis. π: sideways overlap of p orbitals above and below the axis; weaker and more reactive.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'Why are alkenes more reactive than alkanes?', 'The π bond is weaker and has high electron density exposed above and below the plane, attracting electrophiles.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'Describe the mechanism of electrophilic addition of bromine to ethene.', 'The π electrons attack δ+ Br; a carbocation and Br− form; Br− attacks to form 1,2-dibromoethane.', 16),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'What are the benefits and drawbacks of hydrogen as a fuel?', 'Benefits: only produces water and has a high energy per mass. Drawbacks: energy is needed to produce it, and storage and transport are difficult.', 17),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DF', 'Developing Fuels', 'What is meant by a carbon-neutral fuel?', 'A fuel whose combustion releases the same amount of CO2 as was absorbed in producing it (e.g. bioethanol, in theory).', 18),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'ES', 'Elements from the Sea', 'What is the redox reaction in iodine–thiosulfate titrations used for?', 'To find the concentration of an oxidising agent: the agent releases I2 from excess I−, and the I2 is titrated with thiosulfate.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'ES', 'Elements from the Sea', 'Why is starch added near the end point?', 'Adding it too early lets it form an insoluble complex with lots of iodine; near the end point the blue-black to colourless change is sharp.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'ES', 'Elements from the Sea', 'How is bromine extracted from sea water?', 'Acidify and oxidise Br− with Cl2; blow out Br2 with air; react with SO2 to concentrate as HBr; oxidise again with Cl2 and distil.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'ES', 'Elements from the Sea', 'What is the hazard of chlorine gas?', 'It is toxic and irritates the respiratory system. Handle it in a fume cupboard.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'ES', 'Elements from the Sea', 'Give a use of sodium chlorate(I).', 'Household bleach and disinfecting water.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'ES', 'Elements from the Sea', 'Why is hydrogen chloride soluble in water and acidic?', 'It reacts with water to form H3O+ and Cl− ions (fully dissociates), giving hydrochloric acid.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'ES', 'Elements from the Sea', 'What does "oxidising agent" mean?', 'A species that accepts electrons and is itself reduced.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'ES', 'Elements from the Sea', 'Why are the hydrogen halides more easily oxidised down the group?', 'The H–X bond gets weaker down the group and larger halide ions lose electrons more easily, so HI and HBr are stronger reducing agents.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'OZ', 'The Ozone Story', 'What is the difference between UV-A, UV-B and UV-C?', 'UV-C has the highest energy and is completely absorbed by O2 and O3. UV-B damages DNA and is mostly absorbed by ozone. UV-A has the lowest energy and mostly reaches the ground.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'OZ', 'The Ozone Story', 'Why does ozone absorb UV radiation?', 'UV photons have enough energy to break the O–O bond in O3, forming O2 and O atoms.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'OZ', 'The Ozone Story', 'What is the relationship between photon energy and frequency?', 'E = hν.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'OZ', 'The Ozone Story', 'How do nitrogen oxides destroy ozone?', 'NO• + O3 → NO2• + O2, then NO2• + O → NO• + O2; NO acts as a catalyst.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'OZ', 'The Ozone Story', 'Why is the rate of ozone destruction by Cl radicals so high?', 'Cl• is regenerated each cycle, so one radical can destroy thousands of ozone molecules before termination.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'OZ', 'The Ozone Story', 'What are the effects of increased UV reaching Earth?', 'More skin cancer, cataracts and sunburn, and damage to crops and plankton.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'OZ', 'The Ozone Story', 'Define activation enthalpy.', 'The minimum energy colliding particles need to react.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'OZ', 'The Ozone Story', 'How does concentration affect the rate of reaction?', 'Higher concentration means more frequent collisions and so more successful collisions per unit time.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'OZ', 'The Ozone Story', 'What is the effect of pressure on gaseous reaction rates?', 'Higher pressure increases concentration, so collisions are more frequent.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'OZ', 'The Ozone Story', 'How is a halogenoalkane converted into an amine?', 'Heat it with excess concentrated ammonia in ethanol in a sealed tube.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'OZ', 'The Ozone Story', 'What is a nucleophile?', 'A species with a lone pair of electrons that attacks electron-deficient (δ+) carbon atoms.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'OZ', 'The Ozone Story', 'Why does the C–F bond make HFCs unreactive?', 'The C–F bond is very strong, so it is not broken by UV in the stratosphere.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'OZ', 'The Ozone Story', 'What is a greenhouse gas and why are HFCs a concern?', 'A gas that absorbs infrared radiation from Earth. HFCs are powerful greenhouse gases, even though they don''t damage ozone.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'OZ', 'The Ozone Story', 'Why do haloalkane boiling points increase with halogen size?', 'Larger halogens have more electrons, so London forces between molecules are stronger.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'WM', 'What''s in a Medicine?', 'How is an aldehyde distinguished from a ketone using Fehling''s solution?', 'Warm with Fehling''s: an aldehyde gives a brick-red precipitate (Cu2O); a ketone gives no change (stays blue).', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'WM', 'What''s in a Medicine?', 'What reagent tests for a carboxylic acid?', 'Sodium carbonate or hydrogencarbonate: effervescence of CO2.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'WM', 'What''s in a Medicine?', 'How are esters hydrolysed?', 'Heat under reflux with dilute acid (reversible) or NaOH (gives a carboxylate salt and alcohol).', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'WM', 'What''s in a Medicine?', 'What is reflux used for?', 'Heating a reaction mixture for a long time without losing volatile components, which condense and return to the flask.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'WM', 'What''s in a Medicine?', 'How can thin-layer chromatography monitor a reaction?', 'Spot samples of the reaction mixture at intervals alongside the reactant; the reaction is complete when the reactant spot disappears.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'WM', 'What''s in a Medicine?', 'What is the base peak in a mass spectrum?', 'The tallest peak, representing the most abundant (most stable) fragment ion.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'WM', 'What''s in a Medicine?', 'What IR absorption identifies an O–H in an alcohol?', 'A broad absorption at about 3200–3600 cm^-1.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'WM', 'What''s in a Medicine?', 'Why is aspirin a modified natural product?', 'Salicylic acid from willow bark was irritating to the stomach; acetylating the phenol –OH gave aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid), which is less irritating.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'WM', 'What''s in a Medicine?', 'What is the purpose of a melting point test for aspirin?', 'To check purity: pure aspirin melts sharply at about 136 °C; impurities lower and broaden the range.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CI', 'The Chemical Industry', 'What factors are considered when siting a chemical plant?', 'Raw material supply, transport links, workforce, energy supply, water supply, waste disposal and environmental impact.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CI', 'The Chemical Industry', 'Why are many industrial processes run as continuous rather than batch?', 'Continuous processes give a constant output, are easier to automate, and have lower running costs for large volumes.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CI', 'The Chemical Industry', 'How is heat energy recovered in industry?', 'Heat exchangers use heat from exothermic reactions to preheat reactants or generate steam.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CI', 'The Chemical Industry', 'What are the costs of a chemical process?', 'Fixed costs (plant, labour) and variable costs (raw materials, energy, waste treatment).', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CI', 'The Chemical Industry', 'Give the equation and conditions for the Haber process.', 'N2 + 3H2 ⇌ 2NH3, ΔH = −92 kJ mol^-1. Iron catalyst, about 450 °C, about 200 atm.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CI', 'The Chemical Industry', 'Why is ammonia important?', 'It is used to make fertilisers (e.g. ammonium nitrate), nitric acid, explosives and nylon.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CI', 'The Chemical Industry', 'What is the environmental problem with nitrate fertilisers?', 'Run-off into rivers causes eutrophication: algal blooms deplete oxygen, killing aquatic life.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CI', 'The Chemical Industry', 'How does temperature affect Kc for an exothermic reaction?', 'Increasing temperature decreases Kc; the equilibrium shifts in the endothermic (reverse) direction.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CI', 'The Chemical Industry', 'How do you calculate the rate of reaction from a graph?', 'Draw a tangent at the chosen time and find its gradient (change in concentration ÷ change in time).', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'Define lattice enthalpy (formation).', 'The enthalpy change when 1 mole of an ionic solid is formed from its gaseous ions.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'Define enthalpy change of solution.', 'The enthalpy change when 1 mole of solute dissolves completely in water to form an infinitely dilute solution.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'How is enthalpy of solution calculated from lattice and hydration enthalpies?', 'ΔH_solution = −ΔH_lattice + ΣΔH_hydration.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'What factors affect hydration enthalpy?', 'Smaller ions with higher charge have higher charge density, so hydration enthalpy is more exothermic.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'Why do ions dissolve in water?', 'Water molecules are polar: δ− O is attracted to cations and δ+ H to anions, releasing hydration energy.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'What is the total entropy change and when is a process spontaneous?', 'ΔS_total = ΔS_sys + ΔS_surr, where ΔS_surr = −ΔH/T. The process is spontaneous if ΔS_total > 0.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'Why does CO2 dissolve in oceans and how does it form an acid?', 'CO2(aq) + H2O ⇌ H2CO3 ⇌ H+ + HCO3−, which lowers ocean pH.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'Why is ocean acidification a problem for marine life?', 'The higher [H+] reacts with carbonate ions, reducing their availability for shells and coral, and can dissolve CaCO3.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'What is the solubility product Ksp?', 'For a sparingly soluble salt AB: Ksp = [A+][B−] in a saturated solution. It is used to predict precipitation.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'What is the effect of temperature on the solubility of gases in water?', 'Gases are less soluble at higher temperatures, which is why warmer oceans absorb less CO2.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'What is the ionic product of water?', 'Kw = [H+][OH−] = 1.0 × 10^-14 mol^2 dm^-6 at 298 K.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'Calculate the pH of a 0.010 mol dm^-3 NaOH solution.', '[H+] = 10^-14/0.010 = 10^-12, so pH = 12.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'How is the pH of a buffer calculated?', '[H+] = Ka × [HA]/[A−], then pH = −log[H+].', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'Why do buffers only work over a limited range?', 'Once most of the HA or A− is used up, the buffer can no longer absorb added acid or base.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'O', 'Oceans', 'What is the greenhouse effect?', 'Greenhouse gases absorb IR radiated by the Earth, causing bond vibrations, and re-emit it in all directions, warming the lower atmosphere.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'What is a half-cell?', 'An element in contact with a solution of its ions (or two ions of the same element with a Pt electrode), which produces an electrode potential.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'Why is platinum used in some half-cells?', 'It is inert, conducts electricity, and provides a surface for reactions where no solid metal is involved (e.g. Fe3+/Fe2+).', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'What are standard conditions for electrode potentials?', '298 K, 100 kPa and 1.00 mol dm^-3 solutions.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'What is the effect of increasing ion concentration on electrode potential?', 'The equilibrium Mn+ + ne− ⇌ M shifts right, so E becomes more positive.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'How does rusting occur electrochemically?', 'Fe → Fe2+ + 2e− (anode); O2 + 2H2O + 4e− → 4OH− (cathode). Fe(OH)2 forms and is oxidised to hydrated Fe2O3 (rust).', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'How can rusting be prevented?', 'Barrier methods (paint, oil, plastic) or sacrificial protection (zinc, as in galvanising), where the more reactive metal is oxidised instead.', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'What is a ligand?', 'A molecule or ion with a lone pair that forms a dative covalent bond to a central metal ion.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'Define coordination number.', 'The number of dative bonds from ligands to the central metal ion.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'What is a complex ion?', 'A central metal ion surrounded by ligands bonded by dative covalent bonds.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'Why do different ligands produce different colours?', 'Different ligands split the d orbitals by different amounts, changing the frequency of light absorbed.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'What is observed when concentrated HCl is added to [Cu(H2O)6]2+?', 'The blue solution turns yellow-green as [CuCl4]2− forms (a tetrahedral complex).', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'What is the precipitate when NaOH is added to Cu2+?', 'Pale blue Cu(OH)2 (or [Cu(OH)2(H2O)4]).', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'Why is Zn not a transition metal?', 'Its only ion, Zn2+, has a full 3d10 sub-shell (no incomplete d sub-shell).', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'How is a calibration curve used in colorimetry?', 'Measure the absorbance of standard solutions of known concentration, plot absorbance against concentration, then read off the unknown''s concentration.', 14),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'Why is a filter chosen in colorimetry?', 'The filter selects the complementary colour, which the solution absorbs most strongly, giving the greatest sensitivity.', 15),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'Why do transition metals act as catalysts?', 'They can change oxidation state easily and provide surfaces for adsorption of reactants.', 16),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'DM', 'Developing Metals', 'What is the half-equation for dichromate(VI) acting as an oxidising agent?', 'Cr2O7^2− + 14H+ + 6e− → 2Cr3+ + 7H2O (orange to green).', 17),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CD', 'Colour by Design', 'What is a chromophore?', 'The part of a molecule responsible for its colour, usually an extended delocalised system such as –N=N– linking aromatic rings.', 1),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CD', 'Colour by Design', 'What are auxochromes?', 'Groups (e.g. –OH, –NH2) attached to a chromophore that modify the colour and intensity and can improve solubility.', 2),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CD', 'Colour by Design', 'Why must diazotisation be done below 10 °C?', 'Diazonium salts are unstable and decompose above about 10 °C, releasing N2.', 3),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CD', 'Colour by Design', 'What is a coupling agent in azo dye synthesis?', 'A phenol (in alkaline solution) or an aromatic amine that reacts with the diazonium ion by electrophilic substitution.', 4),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CD', 'Colour by Design', 'What is a triglyceride?', 'An ester formed from glycerol (propane-1,2,3-triol) and three fatty acids.', 5),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CD', 'Colour by Design', 'What is hydrogenation of unsaturated oils?', 'Adding H2 across C=C bonds with a nickel catalyst to form more saturated fats (e.g. margarine).', 6),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CD', 'Colour by Design', 'What is the difference between cis and trans fatty acids?', 'Cis: chains bend at the C=C, so they pack poorly (liquid). Trans: straighter chains pack closely; linked to heart disease.', 7),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CD', 'Colour by Design', 'How does Friedel–Crafts acylation modify benzene?', 'An acyl chloride with an AlCl3 catalyst forms an acylium ion (RCO+) electrophile, giving an aromatic ketone.', 8),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CD', 'Colour by Design', 'How does gas chromatography separate compounds?', 'Compounds are carried by an inert gas through a column; they separate by their different solubility in the stationary liquid phase, giving different retention times.', 9),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CD', 'Colour by Design', 'What is retention time?', 'The time from injection to a compound reaching the detector in GC or HPLC.', 10),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CD', 'Colour by Design', 'How can GC be used to find relative amounts of components?', 'The area under each peak is proportional to the amount of that component.', 11),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CD', 'Colour by Design', 'What is the mechanism of reduction of aldehydes with NaBH4?', 'Nucleophilic addition: H− attacks the δ+ carbonyl carbon, and the O− is then protonated by water to form the alcohol.', 12),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CD', 'Colour by Design', 'Why do aromatic compounds with more delocalisation absorb longer wavelengths?', 'A larger delocalised system means the energy gap between orbitals is smaller, so lower-energy (longer-wavelength) visible light is absorbed.', 13),
  ('Chemistry', 'OCR B', 'alevel', 'CD', 'Colour by Design', 'What does the term "hydrolysis of a fat" produce under alkaline conditions?', 'Glycerol and the sodium salts of the fatty acids (soap), a reaction called saponification.', 14)
),
fresh AS (
  SELECT v.* FROM v
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM flashcards f
    WHERE f.subject = v.subject AND f.exam_board = v.exam_board AND f.level = v.level
      AND f.topic_id = v.topic_id AND f.front = v.front)
),
cur AS (
  SELECT subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, count(*) AS n, max(card_order) AS mx
  FROM flashcards
  WHERE subject = 'Chemistry' AND exam_board = 'OCR B' AND level = 'alevel'
  GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4
),
ranked AS (
  SELECT fr.*, coalesce(c.n, 0) AS n, coalesce(c.mx, 0) AS mx,
         row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY fr.topic_id ORDER BY fr.ord) AS rn
  FROM fresh fr
  LEFT JOIN cur c USING (subject, exam_board, level, topic_id)
)
INSERT INTO flashcards (subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, card_order, level)
SELECT subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, mx + rn, level
FROM ranked
WHERE n + rn <= 30;

-- Flashcard top-up: Physics|AQA|alevel — brings each topic up to 30 cards.
-- Generated by scripts/build-flashcard-topup.js from flashcards/topup/src/physics-aqa-alevel.txt
-- Safe to re-run: skips fronts already present and never takes a topic past 30.
WITH v(subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, ord) AS (VALUES
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'What is the resolution of an instrument?', 'The smallest change in the quantity being measured that produces a detectable change in the reading, e.g. 1 mm for a metre rule.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'Give the SI prefixes pico, nano, micro, milli, kilo, mega, giga and tera as powers of ten.', 'p = 10^-12, n = 10^-9, µ = 10^-6, m = 10^-3, k = 10^3, M = 10^6, G = 10^9, T = 10^12.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'How are absolute uncertainties combined when quantities are added or subtracted?', 'The absolute uncertainties are added. E.g. if L = L1 − L2, then ΔL = ΔL1 + ΔL2.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'How is percentage uncertainty calculated from absolute uncertainty?', 'Percentage uncertainty = (absolute uncertainty ÷ measured value) × 100%.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'What is the uncertainty in the mean of a set of repeated readings?', 'Half the range of the readings: (largest − smallest) ÷ 2, ignoring anomalies.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'Why is it better to time 10 oscillations than 1?', 'The absolute uncertainty from human reaction time stays about the same, so dividing the total by 10 reduces the percentage uncertainty in the period tenfold.', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'What is an order-of-magnitude estimate?', 'An approximate value to the nearest power of ten, used to check whether a calculated answer is sensible.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'Estimate the order of magnitude of the mass of an adult human and the height of a door.', 'Mass ~10^2 kg (about 70 kg); door height ~10^0 m (about 2 m).', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'How is the uncertainty in a y-intercept found from a graph?', 'Draw the best-fit line and the worst acceptable line through the error bars; the difference between their y-intercepts gives the uncertainty in the intercept.', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'How are error bars drawn on a graph?', 'A line through each plotted point extending ± the absolute uncertainty in that quantity, in the x and/or y direction.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'Why would a micrometer be chosen over vernier callipers for measuring a wire''s diameter?', 'A micrometer has a finer resolution (0.01 mm vs 0.1 mm or 0.02 mm), giving a smaller percentage uncertainty for small diameters.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'What is a zero error and how is it dealt with?', 'A non-zero reading when the true value is zero (e.g. a micrometer reading 0.02 mm when closed). It is a systematic error: record it and subtract it from every reading.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'How can parallax error be reduced when reading a scale?', 'View the scale at eye level, perpendicular to it, and place the scale as close as possible to the object being measured.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'Give the base units of the volt.', 'V = W A^-1 = J s^-1 A^-1 = kg m^2 s^-3 A^-1.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.1', 'Measurements and Their Errors', 'What is the percentage uncertainty in r if the diameter d = 0.50 ± 0.01 mm?', 'r = d/2 has the same percentage uncertainty as d: 0.01/0.50 × 100 = 2%.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'Why are some nuclei unstable?', 'They have an imbalance of protons and neutrons (or are too large), so the strong force cannot hold them stably and they decay to a more stable configuration.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'Describe how the strong nuclear force varies with separation.', 'Repulsive below about 0.5 fm, attractive up to about 3 fm (strongest near 1 fm), and effectively zero beyond about 3 fm.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'Why was the neutrino hypothesised in beta decay?', 'The emitted beta particles had a continuous range of kinetic energies, so another particle had to carry away the missing energy (and momentum) to conserve energy.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'Write the equation for β+ decay of a proton at the quark level.', 'u → d + e+ + νe (a proton becomes a neutron, emitting a positron and an electron neutrino).', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'What is a meson and give the quark composition of π+ and K+?', 'A hadron made of a quark and an antiquark. π+ = u anti-d; K+ = u anti-s.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'What is strangeness and when is it conserved?', 'A quantum number carried by strange quarks (s has strangeness −1). It is conserved in strong interactions but can change by 0 or ±1 in weak interactions.', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'What do the kaons decay into, and what does this tell you?', 'Kaons decay into pions (and leptons) via the weak interaction, since strangeness changes — shown by their relatively long lifetimes.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'What is the baryon number of a quark, a baryon and a meson?', 'Quark +1/3 (antiquark −1/3); baryon +1; antibaryon −1; meson 0.', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'Which is the only stable baryon?', 'The proton. All other baryons eventually decay into protons.', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'Describe pair production.', 'A photon with energy at least equal to 2mc^2 (the combined rest energy of a particle and its antiparticle) converts into a particle–antiparticle pair, usually near a nucleus to conserve momentum.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'Describe electron capture and the exchange particle involved.', 'A proton captures an inner-shell electron via the weak interaction (W boson exchange): p + e− → n + νe. The proton changes to a neutron and the electron to an electron neutrino.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'What is the work function?', 'The minimum energy needed to remove an electron from the surface of a metal.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'What is the threshold frequency?', 'The minimum frequency of light that will release photoelectrons from a metal: f0 = φ/h.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'What is meant by excitation and ionisation of an atom?', 'Excitation: an electron moves to a higher energy level. Ionisation: an electron gains enough energy to be completely removed from the atom.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.2', 'Particles and Radiation', 'How do line emission spectra provide evidence for discrete energy levels?', 'Each line has a specific frequency, so photons have specific energies hf = E1 − E2, meaning electrons can only occupy fixed energy levels.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'What is the phase difference between two points on a wave one wavelength apart and half a wavelength apart?', 'One wavelength apart: 0 (or 2π rad, in phase). Half a wavelength apart: π rad (180°, antiphase).', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'How does intensity relate to amplitude?', 'Intensity is proportional to amplitude squared: I ∝ A^2.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'What is the difference between a node and an antinode?', 'A node is a point of zero amplitude on a stationary wave; an antinode is a point of maximum amplitude.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'How far apart are adjacent nodes in a stationary wave?', 'Half a wavelength (λ/2).', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'State three differences between stationary and progressive waves.', 'Stationary waves store energy (no net transfer), have varying amplitude with nodes/antinodes, and all points between adjacent nodes are in phase. Progressive waves transfer energy, have constant amplitude, and phase varies continuously.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'State the frequency of the first harmonic on a string of length L, tension T and mass per unit length µ.', 'f = (1/2L)√(T/µ).', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'What is the fundamental wavelength for a pipe closed at one end?', 'λ = 4L: a node at the closed end and an antinode at the open end. Only odd harmonics are present.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'How does the diffraction pattern from a single slit look?', 'A wide, bright central maximum twice the width of the other maxima, with dimmer, narrower side fringes on either side.', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'Why are diffraction gratings better than double slits for measuring wavelength?', 'The maxima are much brighter and sharper and more widely spaced, so their angles can be measured more precisely.', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'How do you find the maximum order visible from a diffraction grating?', 'Set sin θ = 1: n_max = d/λ, rounded down to a whole number.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'State the refractive index of a material in terms of the speed of light.', 'n = c/c_s, where c_s is the speed of light in the material.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'What conditions are needed for total internal reflection?', 'Light travels from a higher to a lower refractive index and the angle of incidence exceeds the critical angle.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'What are material and modal dispersion in optical fibres?', 'Material dispersion: different wavelengths travel at different speeds. Modal dispersion: rays take different paths, so arrive at different times. Both cause pulse broadening.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'How can modal dispersion be reduced?', 'Use a very narrow (single-mode) core so light can take only one path.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.3', 'Waves', 'What happens when unpolarised light passes through two polarising filters at 90° to each other?', 'The first filter polarises the light; the second, crossed at 90°, blocks it completely, so no light gets through.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'How do you resolve a force F at angle θ to the horizontal?', 'Horizontal component = F cos θ; vertical component = F sin θ.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'What is a couple and how is its moment calculated?', 'A pair of equal, opposite, parallel forces not acting along the same line. Moment = one force × perpendicular distance between them.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'What two conditions must be met for an object to be in equilibrium?', 'The resultant force is zero and the resultant moment about any point is zero.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'What is the centre of mass?', 'The single point through which the whole weight of an object can be taken to act.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'What does the gradient and area under a velocity–time graph represent?', 'Gradient = acceleration; area under the graph = displacement.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'Why do horizontal and vertical motion of a projectile get treated separately?', 'They are independent: horizontal velocity is constant (ignoring air resistance) while vertical motion has constant acceleration g.', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'What is terminal velocity?', 'The constant maximum velocity reached when the drag force equals the driving force (e.g. weight), so the resultant force and acceleration are zero.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'State Newton''s second law in terms of momentum.', 'The resultant force equals the rate of change of momentum: F = Δ(mv)/Δt.', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'What does the area under a force–time graph represent?', 'The impulse, which equals the change in momentum.', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'Why do crumple zones and airbags reduce injury?', 'They increase the time over which the momentum changes, so the force (F = Δp/Δt) on the occupants is reduced.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'Define power and give two expressions for it.', 'Power is the rate of doing work: P = ΔW/Δt, and for a constant driving force P = Fv.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'Define efficiency.', 'Efficiency = useful output power (or energy) ÷ input power (or energy), often expressed as a percentage.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'Define density.', 'Mass per unit volume: ρ = m/V, units kg m^-3.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'What does the area between the loading and unloading curves of a rubber band represent?', 'The energy dissipated as heat during one loading–unloading cycle (hysteresis).', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.4', 'Mechanics and Materials', 'Give the combined spring constant of two springs in series and in parallel.', 'Series: 1/k = 1/k1 + 1/k2. Parallel: k = k1 + k2.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'Give the equation linking current to charge-carrier density.', 'I = nAvq, where n is the number density of charge carriers, A the cross-sectional area, v the drift velocity and q the charge per carrier.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'Sketch/describe the I–V characteristic of an ohmic conductor.', 'A straight line through the origin: current is directly proportional to pd at constant temperature.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'Describe the I–V characteristic of a semiconductor diode.', 'Almost no current in reverse bias; in forward bias current rises steeply once the pd exceeds the threshold voltage (about 0.6 V for silicon).', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'Why does the resistance of a metal increase with temperature?', 'The positive ions vibrate more, so the free electrons collide with them more often, reducing the drift velocity for a given pd.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'What is a superconductor?', 'A material whose resistivity falls to zero below its critical temperature, so current can flow with no energy loss.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'Give two uses of superconductors.', 'Strong magnetic fields (MRI scanners, particle accelerators) and lossless power transmission cables.', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'Why does the resistance of an NTC thermistor fall as temperature rises?', 'More charge carriers are released as the temperature rises, which outweighs the increased lattice vibrations.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'State the equation linking EMF, terminal pd and internal resistance.', 'ε = V + Ir = I(R + r), where V is the terminal pd and Ir is the lost volts.', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'What does the gradient and y-intercept of a V–I graph for a cell show?', 'The y-intercept gives the EMF; the gradient is −r (minus the internal resistance).', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'When is maximum power delivered to an external resistor?', 'When the load resistance equals the internal resistance of the source (R = r).', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'What conservation laws are Kirchhoff''s first and second laws based on?', 'First law: conservation of charge. Second law: conservation of energy.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'Give the potential divider equation.', 'V_out = V_in × R2/(R1 + R2), where V_out is taken across R2.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'Describe how to set up a circuit that turns on a light when it gets dark.', 'Put an LDR in a potential divider with a fixed resistor and take V_out across the LDR: in the dark its resistance rises, so V_out rises and switches on the lamp circuit.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'What is the kilowatt-hour?', 'A unit of energy: the energy transferred by a 1 kW device in 1 hour = 3.6 × 10^6 J.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.5', 'Electricity', 'What is the total resistance of three 6 Ω resistors in parallel?', '1/R = 1/6 + 1/6 + 1/6 = 1/2, so R = 2 Ω.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'State the relationship between linear speed and angular speed.', 'v = ωr.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'State the centripetal force formula in two forms.', 'F = mv^2/r = mω^2r, directed towards the centre of the circle.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'Why is an object moving in a circle at constant speed accelerating?', 'Its direction, and so its velocity, is continuously changing; the acceleration is directed towards the centre.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'At what point in a vertical loop is the tension in a string greatest?', 'At the bottom, where T = mg + mv^2/r (tension must supply the centripetal force as well as balance weight).', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'State the defining equation of SHM.', 'a = −ω^2x: acceleration is proportional to displacement and directed towards the equilibrium position.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'What is the phase relationship between displacement, velocity and acceleration in SHM?', 'Velocity leads displacement by π/2; acceleration is in antiphase with displacement (π out of phase).', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'Describe the energy changes in an undamped oscillator.', 'Energy is continuously transferred between kinetic and potential; total energy is constant. KE is maximum at equilibrium, PE is maximum at maximum displacement.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'Write the maximum acceleration in SHM.', 'a_max = ω^2A.', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'What is critical damping?', 'Damping that returns the system to equilibrium in the shortest possible time without oscillating (e.g. car suspension).', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'How does damping affect a resonance curve?', 'More damping lowers the peak amplitude, broadens the peak and shifts the resonant frequency slightly lower.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'Define internal energy.', 'The sum of the randomly distributed kinetic and potential energies of all the particles in a system.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'What is absolute zero?', '0 K (−273.15 °C): the temperature at which particles have minimum internal energy.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'State Boyle''s law, Charles''s law and the pressure law.', 'Boyle: pV = constant (constant T). Charles: V/T = constant (constant p). Pressure law: p/T = constant (constant V).', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'State the kinetic theory equation for pressure.', 'pV = (1/3)Nm(c_rms)^2.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.6', 'Further Mechanics and Thermal Physics', 'What is the Boltzmann constant in terms of R and N_A?', 'k = R/N_A ≈ 1.38 × 10^-23 J K^-1.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'Describe the gravitational field lines around a point mass.', 'Radial lines pointing inwards towards the mass; closer together nearer the mass, showing a stronger field.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'State the formula for gravitational potential at distance r from a mass M.', 'V = −GM/r. It is zero at infinity and negative everywhere else.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'What is the link between gravitational field strength and potential gradient?', 'g = −ΔV/Δr: field strength is minus the potential gradient.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'What are equipotentials?', 'Surfaces or lines joining points of equal potential. No work is done moving along them, and they are always perpendicular to field lines.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'Derive the orbital speed of a satellite.', 'Set GMm/r^2 = mv^2/r, so v = √(GM/r).', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'Give the uses of low (polar) orbits versus geostationary orbits.', 'Low orbits: imaging, weather, surveillance (they pass over the whole Earth). Geostationary: communications and TV (they stay above one point).', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'State the electric field strength in a uniform field between parallel plates.', 'E = V/d, directed from the positive to the negative plate.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'State the electric potential at distance r from a point charge.', 'V = Q/(4πε0r).', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'Describe the path of a charged particle entering a uniform electric field at right angles.', 'A parabola: constant velocity parallel to the plates and constant acceleration perpendicular to them, towards the oppositely charged plate.', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'Define the time constant of an RC circuit.', 'τ = RC: the time for the charge (or pd or current) to fall to 1/e (about 37%) of its initial value during discharge.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'State the capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor.', 'C = Aε0εr/d.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'What is the force on a charge moving in a magnetic field?', 'F = BQv (perpendicular to both v and B), which makes the particle move in a circle of radius r = mv/(BQ).', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'Define magnetic flux and flux linkage.', 'Flux Φ = BA (for B perpendicular to A), unit weber. Flux linkage = NΦ for a coil of N turns.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'State the EMF induced in a coil rotating in a uniform field.', 'ε = BANω sin ωt.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.7', 'Fields and Their Consequences', 'Why do transformers use a laminated iron core?', 'Laminations reduce eddy currents in the core, cutting energy losses from heating.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'What conclusions came from Rutherford scattering?', 'Most of the atom is empty space; the nucleus is tiny, dense and positively charged, since a few α particles were deflected through large angles.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'What is background radiation and give two sources?', 'Radiation present all the time from natural and artificial sources, e.g. radon gas, rocks, cosmic rays and medical X-rays.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'State the inverse square law for gamma radiation.', 'I = k/x^2: intensity is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from a point source.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'How is the nuclear radius estimated using electron diffraction?', 'High-energy electrons diffract around a nucleus. The angle of the first minimum gives R from sin θ = 0.61λ/R.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'Why is nuclear density roughly the same for all nuclei?', 'R = r0A^(1/3), so volume ∝ A, and mass ∝ A; mass ÷ volume is constant (about 10^17 kg m^-3).', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'State the equation linking activity and number of nuclei.', 'A = λN.', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'Write the exponential decay equation for number of undecayed nuclei.', 'N = N0 e^(−λt).', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'What is a metastable state?', 'An excited nuclear state with a relatively long lifetime that decays by gamma emission, e.g. technetium-99m used as a medical tracer.', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'Why is technetium-99m suitable as a medical tracer?', 'It emits only gamma rays (low ionisation, detectable outside the body), has a 6-hour half-life and can be made on site.', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'What is the unified atomic mass unit and its energy equivalent?', '1 u = 1/12 the mass of a carbon-12 atom ≈ 1.661 × 10^-27 kg ≈ 931.5 MeV.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'Describe a chain reaction.', 'Each fission of U-235 releases 2–3 neutrons, which go on to cause further fissions, so the reaction becomes self-sustaining.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'What is critical mass?', 'The minimum mass of fuel needed for a self-sustaining chain reaction.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'Why does the moderator slow neutrons?', 'Slow (thermal) neutrons are much more likely to be absorbed by U-235 and cause fission. The moderator slows them by elastic collisions.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'What safety features are used in a nuclear reactor?', 'Thick concrete shielding, emergency shutdown (control rods dropped fully in), and remote handling of spent fuel.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'alevel', '3.8', 'Nuclear Physics', 'Where does the energy released in fission and fusion come from?', 'The products have a higher binding energy per nucleon, so there is a loss in mass and the mass difference is released as energy (E = mc^2).', 15)
),
fresh AS (
  SELECT v.* FROM v
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (
    SELECT 1 FROM flashcards f
    WHERE f.subject = v.subject AND f.exam_board = v.exam_board AND f.level = v.level
      AND f.topic_id = v.topic_id AND f.front = v.front)
),
cur AS (
  SELECT subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, count(*) AS n, max(card_order) AS mx
  FROM flashcards
  WHERE subject = 'Physics' AND exam_board = 'AQA' AND level = 'alevel'
  GROUP BY 1, 2, 3, 4
),
ranked AS (
  SELECT fr.*, coalesce(c.n, 0) AS n, coalesce(c.mx, 0) AS mx,
         row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY fr.topic_id ORDER BY fr.ord) AS rn
  FROM fresh fr
  LEFT JOIN cur c USING (subject, exam_board, level, topic_id)
)
INSERT INTO flashcards (subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, card_order, level)
SELECT subject, exam_board, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, mx + rn, level
FROM ranked
WHERE n + rn <= 30;

-- Flashcard top-up: Physics|AQA|gcse — brings each topic up to 30 cards.
-- Generated by scripts/build-flashcard-topup.js from flashcards/topup/src/physics-aqa-gcse.txt
-- Safe to re-run: skips fronts already present and never takes a topic past 30.
WITH v(subject, exam_board, level, topic_id, topic_name, front, back, ord) AS (VALUES
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'Name the energy stores.', 'Kinetic, gravitational potential, elastic potential, thermal, chemical, magnetic, electrostatic and nuclear.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'Name four ways energy can be transferred.', 'Mechanically (by forces), electrically, by heating, and by radiation (light and sound).', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What is the equation for kinetic energy?', 'KE = ½mv².', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What is the equation for gravitational potential energy?', 'GPE = mgh.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'Calculate the KE of a 2 kg ball moving at 3 m/s.', '½ × 2 × 3² = 9 J.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'Calculate the GPE gained by a 5 kg mass lifted 2 m (g = 10 N/kg).', '5 × 10 × 2 = 100 J.', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What is the law of conservation of energy?', 'Energy can''t be created or destroyed, only transferred between stores.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What is wasted energy?', 'Energy transferred to stores that aren''t useful, usually thermal energy in the surroundings.', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What is efficiency?', 'Useful energy output ÷ total energy input (× 100 for a percentage).', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'A motor uses 200 J and transfers 150 J usefully. What is its efficiency?', '150 ÷ 200 = 0.75, or 75%.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'How can energy waste be reduced?', 'Lubrication to reduce friction, and insulation to reduce heat loss.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What is thermal conductivity?', 'How quickly a material transfers energy by heating through conduction.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'How does the thickness of a wall affect heat loss?', 'Thicker walls reduce the rate of heat loss.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What is a Sankey diagram?', 'A diagram showing energy transfers, with arrow widths proportional to the amount of energy.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What are non-renewable energy resources?', 'Resources that will run out, e.g. coal, oil, gas and nuclear fuel.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What are renewable energy resources?', 'Resources that will be replenished, e.g. solar, wind, hydroelectric, tidal, geothermal, biofuel and wave.', 16),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of fossil fuels.', 'Advantage: reliable and can meet demand. Disadvantage: produce CO₂ and will run out.', 17),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of wind power.', 'Advantage: renewable, no CO₂ during operation. Disadvantage: unreliable and can be noisy or unsightly.', 18),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of solar power.', 'Advantage: renewable, no emissions. Disadvantage: no power at night and less in cloudy weather.', 19),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of nuclear power.', 'Advantage: no CO₂ and reliable. Disadvantage: radioactive waste and risk of accidents.', 20),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What is work done?', 'Energy transferred when a force moves an object: W = F × d.', 21),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What is the unit of work done?', 'The joule (J).', 22),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'Calculate the work done when a 20 N force moves an object 5 m.', '20 × 5 = 100 J.', 23),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What is power?', 'The rate of doing work (energy transferred per second): P = E/t.', 24),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'Calculate the power of a motor that transfers 600 J in 20 s.', '600 ÷ 20 = 30 W.', 25),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What is 1 watt?', '1 joule of energy transferred per second.', 26),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'Why is work done against friction wasted?', 'It transfers energy to thermal stores, heating the surroundings.', 27),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What happens to energy when you push a box along the floor?', 'Work is done against friction, transferring energy to thermal energy.', 28),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'What happens to energy when a car brakes?', 'Kinetic energy is transferred to thermal energy in the brakes.', 29),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.1', 'Energy', 'How is work done related to energy transfer?', 'Work done equals energy transferred.', 30),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is electric current?', 'The rate of flow of charge: I = Q/t.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is potential difference?', 'The energy transferred per unit charge between two points: V = E/Q.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is resistance?', 'How much a component opposes the flow of current: R = V/I.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is Ohm''s law?', 'The current through a resistor is directly proportional to the potential difference, at constant temperature.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'Calculate the current when 12 V is applied across a 4 Ω resistor.', 'I = 12 ÷ 4 = 3 A.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'Calculate the charge that flows when 2 A flows for 30 s.', 'Q = 2 × 30 = 60 C.', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is a series circuit?', 'A circuit where components are connected in a single loop.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is a parallel circuit?', 'A circuit where components are connected on separate branches.', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What happens to current in a series circuit?', 'It is the same everywhere.', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What happens to potential difference in a series circuit?', 'It is shared between the components.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What happens to current in a parallel circuit?', 'It is shared between the branches; the total current equals the sum of the branch currents.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What happens to potential difference in a parallel circuit?', 'It is the same across each branch.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'How do you calculate total resistance in series?', 'Add the resistances: R_total = R₁ + R₂.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What happens to total resistance when resistors are added in parallel?', 'It decreases, and is less than the smallest individual resistance.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'How is an ammeter connected?', 'In series with the component.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'How is a voltmeter connected?', 'In parallel across the component.', 16),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is the I–V graph for a fixed resistor?', 'A straight line through the origin.', 17),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is the I–V graph for a filament lamp?', 'A curve that gets less steep, because resistance increases as the filament heats up.', 18),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is the I–V graph for a diode?', 'Current flows in one direction only, after a small threshold voltage.', 19),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'How does the resistance of a thermistor change?', 'It decreases as temperature increases.', 20),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'How does the resistance of an LDR change?', 'It decreases as light intensity increases.', 21),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is electrical power?', 'P = IV = I²R.', 22),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'Calculate the power of a lamp with 230 V and 0.5 A.', 'P = 230 × 0.5 = 115 W.', 23),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is the equation for energy transferred electrically?', 'E = IVt (or E = QV).', 24),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is the difference between AC and DC?', 'AC changes direction regularly; DC flows in one direction only.', 25),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is the UK mains supply?', 'AC at 230 V and 50 Hz.', 26),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What are the three wires in a UK plug?', 'Live (brown), neutral (blue) and earth (green and yellow).', 27),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is the purpose of the earth wire?', 'It stops the casing becoming live, preventing electric shocks if there is a fault.', 28),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'What is the purpose of a fuse?', 'It melts and breaks the circuit if the current is too high, protecting the appliance and preventing fires.', 29),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.2', 'Electricity', 'Why does a current in a resistor cause heating?', 'Electrons collide with ions in the lattice, transferring energy and making them vibrate more.', 30),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is density?', 'Mass per unit volume: ρ = m/V.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is the unit of density?', 'kg/m³ (or g/cm³).', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'Calculate the density of a 200 g block with volume 100 cm³.', '200 ÷ 100 = 2 g/cm³.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'How can the density of an irregular object be found?', 'Measure its mass with a balance and its volume by displacement of water in a measuring cylinder.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is the particle arrangement in a solid?', 'Particles are closely packed in a regular pattern, vibrating about fixed positions.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is the particle arrangement in a liquid?', 'Particles are close together but randomly arranged and can move past each other.', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is the particle arrangement in a gas?', 'Particles are far apart, randomly arranged and move quickly in all directions.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'Why are gases less dense than solids?', 'The particles are much further apart.', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is internal energy?', 'The total kinetic and potential energy of all the particles in a system.', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What happens to internal energy when a substance is heated?', 'It increases, either raising the temperature or changing the state.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is specific heat capacity?', 'The energy needed to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 °C.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is the equation for specific heat capacity?', 'ΔQ = m × c × Δθ.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'Calculate the energy needed to heat 2 kg of water by 10 °C (c = 4200 J/kg°C).', '2 × 4200 × 10 = 84,000 J.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is specific latent heat?', 'The energy needed to change the state of 1 kg of a substance without a change in temperature.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is the equation for specific latent heat?', 'Q = m × L.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is the specific latent heat of fusion?', 'The energy needed to change 1 kg from solid to liquid.', 16),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is the specific latent heat of vaporisation?', 'The energy needed to change 1 kg from liquid to gas.', 17),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'Why does temperature stay constant during a change of state?', 'The energy is used to break bonds between particles, not to increase kinetic energy.', 18),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is melting?', 'Changing from solid to liquid.', 19),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is evaporation?', 'Changing from liquid to gas at the surface of a liquid, below its boiling point.', 20),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is condensation?', 'Changing from gas to liquid.', 21),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is sublimation?', 'Changing directly from solid to gas.', 22),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'Are changes of state physical or chemical?', 'Physical, because they are reversible and the substance keeps its chemical properties.', 23),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'Is mass conserved when a substance changes state?', 'Yes.', 24),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'How do gas particles exert pressure?', 'They collide with the walls of the container, exerting a force.', 25),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What happens to gas pressure when temperature increases (constant volume)?', 'Pressure increases because particles move faster and collide more often and with more force.', 26),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is absolute zero?', '−273 °C (0 K), the lowest possible temperature, where particles have the minimum kinetic energy.', 27),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'Convert 27 °C to kelvin.', '27 + 273 = 300 K.', 28),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What happens to gas pressure when volume decreases (constant temperature)?', 'Pressure increases because particles hit the walls more often.', 29),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.3', 'Particle Model of Matter', 'What is the equation linking pressure and volume at constant temperature?', 'p₁V₁ = p₂V₂.', 30),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is the structure of an atom?', 'A small, dense, positively charged nucleus containing protons and neutrons, surrounded by electrons.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What are the relative charges of protons, neutrons and electrons?', 'Proton +1, neutron 0, electron −1.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What are the relative masses of protons, neutrons and electrons?', 'Proton 1, neutron 1, electron about 1/1840.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is the atomic number?', 'The number of protons in the nucleus.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is the mass number?', 'The total number of protons and neutrons in the nucleus.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is an isotope?', 'Atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is radioactive decay?', 'The random process in which an unstable nucleus emits radiation to become more stable.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is an alpha particle?', 'Two protons and two neutrons (a helium nucleus).', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is a beta-minus particle?', 'A fast-moving electron emitted from the nucleus when a neutron turns into a proton.', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is a positron?', 'A particle with the same mass as an electron but a positive charge, emitted in beta-plus decay.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is gamma radiation?', 'High-frequency electromagnetic radiation emitted from the nucleus.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is the penetrating power of alpha, beta and gamma?', 'Alpha is stopped by paper, beta by a few mm of aluminium, gamma is reduced by thick lead or concrete.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is the ionising power of alpha, beta and gamma?', 'Alpha is highly ionising, beta moderately, gamma weakly.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What happens in alpha decay?', 'The mass number decreases by 4 and the atomic number decreases by 2.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What happens in beta-minus decay?', 'The mass number stays the same and the atomic number increases by 1.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What happens in gamma emission?', 'No change to mass number or atomic number; the nucleus loses energy.', 16),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is half-life?', 'The time taken for the number of radioactive nuclei (or activity) to halve.', 17),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is activity?', 'The rate at which a source decays, measured in becquerels (Bq).', 18),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'A sample has activity 800 Bq and half-life 2 hours. What is the activity after 6 hours?', 'Three half-lives: 800 → 400 → 200 → 100 Bq.', 19),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is background radiation?', 'Radiation that is always around us from natural and man-made sources.', 20),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'Name three sources of background radiation.', 'Radon gas, rocks, cosmic rays, food, and medical X-rays.', 21),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is irradiation?', 'Exposing an object to radiation; the object does not become radioactive.', 22),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is contamination?', 'When radioactive atoms get onto or into an object.', 23),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'How can workers be protected from radiation?', 'Shielding, keeping distance, limiting exposure time, and monitoring doses.', 24),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'How is radiation used in medicine?', 'Radiotherapy to kill cancer cells, tracers to diagnose problems, and sterilising equipment.', 25),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What did the plum pudding model suggest?', 'The atom was a ball of positive charge with electrons embedded in it.', 26),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What did the alpha scattering experiment show?', 'Most of the atom is empty space and there is a small, dense, positive nucleus.', 27),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'How did Bohr improve the atomic model?', 'He suggested electrons orbit the nucleus in fixed energy levels (shells).', 28),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What happens when an electron moves to a higher energy level?', 'It absorbs electromagnetic radiation.', 29),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.4', 'Atomic Structure', 'What is nuclear fission?', 'Splitting a large nucleus into smaller nuclei, releasing energy and neutrons.', 30),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is a resultant force?', 'The single force that has the same effect as all the forces acting on an object.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'How do you calculate the resultant of two forces in the same direction?', 'Add them.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'How do you calculate the resultant of two forces in opposite directions?', 'Subtract the smaller from the larger; the resultant acts in the direction of the larger.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What happens if the resultant force is zero?', 'The object stays at rest or continues at constant velocity.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is a contact force?', 'A force that acts when objects are touching, e.g. friction, air resistance, tension and normal contact force.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is a non-contact force?', 'A force that acts at a distance, e.g. gravity, magnetism and electrostatic force.', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is a free-body force diagram?', 'A diagram showing all the forces acting on a single object, with arrows showing size and direction.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'How can forces be resolved?', 'By splitting a force into two perpendicular components, e.g. horizontal and vertical.', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is a moment?', 'The turning effect of a force: moment = force × perpendicular distance from the pivot.', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is the unit of a moment?', 'Newton metre (N m).', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is speed?', 'The distance travelled per unit time: speed = distance ÷ time.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is velocity?', 'Speed in a given direction.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is acceleration?', 'The rate of change of velocity: a = (v − u) ÷ t.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is the unit of acceleration?', 'm/s².', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What does the gradient of a distance–time graph show?', 'Speed.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What does a horizontal line on a distance–time graph show?', 'The object is stationary.', 16),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What does the gradient of a velocity–time graph show?', 'Acceleration.', 17),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What does the area under a velocity–time graph show?', 'Distance travelled.', 18),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What does a horizontal line on a velocity–time graph show?', 'Constant velocity.', 19),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is the equation linking v, u, a and x?', 'v² − u² = 2ax.', 20),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'A car accelerates from 10 m/s to 30 m/s in 5 s. What is its acceleration?', '(30 − 10) ÷ 5 = 4 m/s².', 21),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is the acceleration due to gravity near Earth''s surface?', 'About 10 m/s² (9.8 m/s²).', 22),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'Give typical speeds for walking, cycling and a car in town.', 'Walking about 1.5 m/s, cycling about 6 m/s, a car in town about 13 m/s.', 23),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is Newton''s first law?', 'An object stays at rest or moves at constant velocity unless a resultant force acts on it.', 24),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is elastic deformation?', 'When an object returns to its original shape after the force is removed.', 25),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is inelastic (plastic) deformation?', 'When an object doesn''t return to its original shape after the force is removed.', 26),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is Hooke''s law?', 'The extension of a spring is directly proportional to the force applied, up to the limit of proportionality.', 27),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is the equation for Hooke''s law?', 'F = k × x.', 28),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'What is the spring constant?', 'The force needed to stretch a spring by 1 m, measured in N/m.', 29),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.5', 'Forces', 'Calculate the force needed to stretch a spring with k = 50 N/m by 0.2 m.', '50 × 0.2 = 10 N.', 30),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What is a wave?', 'A disturbance that transfers energy from one place to another without transferring matter.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What is a transverse wave?', 'A wave where the vibrations are at right angles to the direction of energy transfer, e.g. light.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What is a longitudinal wave?', 'A wave where the vibrations are parallel to the direction of energy transfer, e.g. sound.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What is amplitude?', 'The maximum displacement of a point on the wave from its rest position.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What is wavelength?', 'The distance from one point on a wave to the same point on the next wave.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What is frequency?', 'The number of waves passing a point each second, measured in hertz (Hz).', 6),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What is the period of a wave?', 'The time for one complete wave to pass a point: T = 1/f.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What is the wave equation?', 'Wave speed = frequency × wavelength (v = fλ).', 8),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'A wave has frequency 50 Hz and wavelength 2 m. What is its speed?', '50 × 2 = 100 m/s.', 9),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What is the equation linking speed, distance and time for waves?', 'v = x/t.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What are compressions and rarefactions?', 'Regions in a longitudinal wave where particles are close together (compressions) and spread apart (rarefactions).', 11),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What is reflection?', 'When a wave bounces off a surface.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What is refraction?', 'The change in direction of a wave as it passes from one medium to another, due to a change in speed.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'Why do waves refract?', 'They change speed when entering a different medium.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What happens to light entering glass from air?', 'It slows down and bends towards the normal.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What is the normal?', 'An imaginary line at 90° to the surface where the wave hits.', 16),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'How can the speed of sound in air be measured?', 'Measure the time for sound to travel a known distance, e.g. using an echo or two microphones, then use v = d/t.', 17),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'How can the speed of water waves be measured in a ripple tank?', 'Measure the wavelength using a strobe or ruler and count the frequency, then use v = fλ.', 18),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What is the electromagnetic spectrum?', 'A continuous range of transverse waves that travel at the speed of light in a vacuum.', 19),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'List the EM spectrum in order of increasing frequency.', 'Radio, microwaves, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays, gamma rays.', 20),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What is the speed of EM waves in a vacuum?', '3 × 10⁸ m/s.', 21),
  ('Physics', 'AQA', 'gcse', '4.6', 'Waves', 'What are the uses of radio waves?', 'Broadcasting TV and radio, and communications.', 22),
